it-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/input.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":56,\"content\":\"     * Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise\"},{\"line_number\":94,\"content\":\" * - Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\"},{\"line_number\":163,\"content\":\"    gamepadDeadzone: number;\"},{\"line_number\":203,\"content\":\"    wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean;\"},{\"line_number\":232,\"content\":\"    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/tween.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":3,\"content\":\" * pads. Game time (they pause, slow down and freeze with `engine.timeScale` and hitstops)\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/snapshot.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":9,\"content\":\" *   engine.step(300);                             // ...play on\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/particles/pool.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":72,\"content\":\"    step(dt: number): void;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/replay.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":5,\"content\":\" * one back frame for frame, and `engine.fingerprint()` hashes the state, so two runs that\"},{\"line_number\":10,\"content\":\" *   engine.fingerprint() === fingerprintAtTheEnd;            // the same game, frame for frame\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/Engine.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":95,\"content\":\"     * The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives\"},{\"line_number\":100,\"content\":\"    fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string;\"},{\"line_number\":239,\"content\":\"     * frame keeps rendering so the picture stays up. Fix the first one first. (`step()` throws\"},{\"line_number\":257,\"content\":\"    /** Game time from real time: the speed and hitstops (`timeScale`, `hitstop()`). */\"},{\"line_number\":278,\"content\":\"    /** True once the first start has finished: `step()` before it is a tool's race, and throws. */\"},{\"line_number\":288,\"content\":\"     * Tooling: the render loop stops driving the game; advance it yourself with `step()`.\"},{\"line_number\":347,\"content\":\"    get timeScale(): number;\"},{\"line_number\":348,\"content\":\"    set timeScale(v: number);\"},{\"line_number\":385,\"content\":\"     * console. It runs in manual time from the start: advance it with `step()`, give it input with\"},{\"line_number\":390,\"content\":\"    static headless(game: Game, options?: HeadlessOptions): Promise<Engine>;\"},{\"line_number\":429,\"content\":\"        timeScale: number;\"},{\"line_number\":484,\"content\":\"    step(n?: number, dt?: number): void;\"},{\"line_number\":486,\"content\":\"     * One manual frame (`step()`), its input live or from a recorded frame (`replay()`). A game\"},{\"line_number\":487,\"content\":\"     * hook's exception finishes the frame first, then is thrown to the caller of `step()`; in a\"},{\"line_number\":544,\"content\":\"    /** Keep this frame's input in the level's recording (live play and `step()`, not replays). */\"},{\"line_number\":555,\"content\":\"     * (`step()`), waiting for promise callbacks wherever the recorded run did. `frames` stops\"},{\"line_number\":556,\"content\":\"     * early. Afterwards the level is where the recorded one was (`fingerprint()` matches if the\"},{\"line_number\":557,\"content\":\"     * game is deterministic) and stays in manual time: `step()` plays on from there, live, and\"},{\"line_number\":585,\"content\":\"     * the game's own (`Game.fingerprint`). Two runs that should be the same (a replay) compare equal. Not the `status()` line: a\"},{\"line_number\":588,\"content\":\"    fingerprint(): string;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/clock.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":2,\"content\":\" * Game time from real time: a speed (`engine.timeScale`: slow motion, fast forward, 0 to\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/character/states.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":25,\"content\":\"    step(c: PlatformerCharacter, dt: number, input: MoveInput): void;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/render/lights.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":70,\"content\":\"    step(dt: number, items: readonly T[]): AssignStats;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/physics/buoyancy.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":11,\"content\":\" *   // per fixed step, before the physics step (a game's fixedUpdate): floaters.step(dt)\"},{\"line_number\":54,\"content\":\"    step(dt: number): void;\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b36c83f6-330c-48d8-81d3-a7389435c6e5-22","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Pixel Engine: models and textures\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. Read `GUIDE.md` first. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures, `CREDITS.md` where they came from.\\n\\nFor an agent building a game on the kit; read `GUIDE.md` first. Objects and pixel-art textures\\nare **plain data** you write, check and look at, then fix: a `ModelDef` of keyed primitives that\\nthe engine turns into toon meshes, a `TextureDef` of layers it bakes into texels. No modelling\\ntool and no image files. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures and the imported\\npacks (`library.json` holds the same as data), `check.mjs --sheets` draws what you write, and `examples/market.html` is a scene built this way\\n10→(section 6). Every name here comes from `./pixel-engine.js`; `API.md` has the signatures.\\n\\nContents: 1 The loop · 2 Models · 3 Textures · 4 Style rules · 5 In a game · 6 The example ·\\n7 Credits\\n\\n## 1. The loop\\n\\n1. **Start from the library.** Search `LIBRARY.md` (by family, then each pack; id, size, params,\\n   palette slots, tags, one row each, a model's variants like `rock-large-a` … `-f` sharing\\n   one), filter `library.json`, or search in code:\\n20→   `MODEL_LIBRARY.search('wooden chair')` ranks id words over tags over `about`, plurals and\\n   parts of words included; `MODEL_LIBRARY.get('fence')` takes an id. Place library models as\\n   they are, re-coloured or with other params, and `use` them inside your own: a stall with\\n   crates is a few lines.\\n2. **Write** your defs in a module of their own (`models.js`) that exports them; the game\\n   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\\n3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\\n   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\\n   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\\n   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\\n30→4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\\n   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\\n   unless you mean it.\\n5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\\n   renderer, with lights and looks.\\n\\nIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\\n`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\\nsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\\n\\n40→| sheet row | answers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\\n| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\\n| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\\n| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\\n\\nSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\\ngame is the truth.\\n\\n50→## 2. Models\\n\\n```js\\nexport const crate = model({\\n  id: 'crate',\\n  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\\n  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\\n  parts: [\\n    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\\n60→    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\\n    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\\n  ],\\n});\\n```\\n\\nMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\\npositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\\nabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\\n\\n70→- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\\n  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\\n  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\\n- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\\n  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\\n  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\\n- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\\n  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\\n  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\\n\\n80→**Composition:**\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Pixel Engine: models and textures\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. Read `GUIDE.md` first. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures, `CREDITS.md` where they came from.\\n\\nFor an agent building a game on the kit; read `GUIDE.md` first. Objects and pixel-art textures\\nare **plain data** you write, check and look at, then fix: a `ModelDef` of keyed primitives that\\nthe engine turns into toon meshes, a `TextureDef` of layers it bakes into texels. No modelling\\ntool and no image files. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures and the imported\\npacks (`library.json` holds the same as data), `check.mjs --sheets` draws what you write, and `examples/market.html` is a scene built this way\\n10→(section 6). Every name here comes from `./pixel-engine.js`; `API.md` has the signatures.\\n\\nContents: 1 The loop · 2 Models · 3 Textures · 4 Style rules · 5 In a game · 6 The example ·\\n7 Credits\\n\\n## 1. The loop\\n\\n1. **Start from the library.** Search `LIBRARY.md` (by family, then each pack; id, size, params,\\n   palette slots, tags, one row each, a model's variants like `rock-large-a` … `-f` sharing\\n   one), filter `library.json`, or search in code:\\n20→   `MODEL_LIBRARY.search('wooden chair')` ranks id words over tags over `about`, plurals and\\n   parts of words included; `MODEL_LIBRARY.get('fence')` takes an id. Place library models as\\n   they are, re-coloured or with other params, and `use` them inside your own: a stall with\\n   crates is a few lines.\\n2. **Write** your defs in a module of their own (`models.js`) that exports them; the game\\n   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\\n3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\\n   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\\n   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\\n   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\\n30→4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\\n   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\\n   unless you mean it.\\n5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\\n   renderer, with lights and looks.\\n\\nIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\\n`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\\nsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\\n\\n40→| sheet row | answers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\\n| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\\n| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\\n| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\\n\\nSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\\ngame is the truth.\\n\\n50→## 2. Models\\n\\n```js\\nexport const crate = model({\\n  id: 'crate',\\n  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\\n  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\\n  parts: [\\n    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\\n60→    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\\n    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\\n  ],\\n});\\n```\\n\\nMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\\npositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\\nabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\\n\\n70→- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\\n  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\\n  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\\n- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\\n  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\\n  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\\n- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\\n  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\\n  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\\n\\n80→**Composition:**\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/ASSETS.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"# Pixel Engine: models and textures\\n\\n> Pixel Engine **0.46.0**, build 2026-11-02 f75d4a6. Read `GUIDE.md` first. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures, `CREDITS.md` where they came from.\\n\\nFor an agent building a game on the kit; read `GUIDE.md` first. Objects and pixel-art textures\\nare **plain data** you write, check and look at, then fix: a `ModelDef` of keyed primitives that\\nthe engine turns into toon meshes, a `TextureDef` of layers it bakes into texels. No modelling\\ntool and no image files. `LIBRARY.md` lists the built-in models and textures and the imported\\npacks (`library.json` holds the same as data), `check.mjs --sheets` draws what you write, and `examples/market.html` is a scene built this way\\n(section 6). Every name here comes from `./pixel-engine.js`; `API.md` has the signatures.\\n\\nContents: 1 The loop · 2 Models · 3 Textures · 4 Style rules · 5 In a game · 6 The example ·\\n7 Credits\\n\\n## 1. The loop\\n\\n1. **Start from the library.** Search `LIBRARY.md` (by family, then each pack; id, size, params,\\n   palette slots, tags, one row each, a model's variants like `rock-large-a` … `-f` sharing\\n   one), filter `library.json`, or search in code:\\n   `MODEL_LIBRARY.search('wooden chair')` ranks id words over tags over `about`, plurals and\\n   parts of words included; `MODEL_LIBRARY.get('fence')` takes an id. Place library models as\\n   they are, re-coloured or with other params, and `use` them inside your own: a stall with\\n   crates is a few lines.\\n2. **Write** your defs in a module of their own (`models.js`) that exports them; the game\\n   imports it. Keep the engine start out of that module.\\n3. **Check and look:** `node check.mjs --sheets models.js` draws a sheet of every def the module\\n   exports (by name, in an array or object, or in a catalog: `check/sheets/<id>.png`; textures\\n   `<id>.tex.png`) and prints its problems and warnings; it exits 1 on a problem. **Open the\\n   PNGs.** `--sheets fence,cobbles` draws library ids: look at one before you place it.\\n4. **Fix** the numbers the messages and the sheet point at, and run it again until there is no\\n   problem and the game-scale previews read. Warnings name what won't read at 480×270: fix them\\n   unless you mean it.\\n5. **Look in the game:** `node check.mjs index.html --cameras`, then the PNGs: the real\\n   renderer, with lights and looks.\\n\\nIn code, `checkModel(def, { catalog })` and `validateTexture(def)` return\\n`{ problems, warnings }`, and `modelSheet(def, { catalog })` and `textureSheet(def)` return the\\nsheet as RGBA pixels (`image`) with them.\\n\\n| sheet row | answers |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| turntable, 8 angles at one scale | Is the silhouette right from every side? |\\n| front / side / top on a 0.1 m grid, with the size in metres | Are the parts where the numbers say? Do they touch? Is it the right size? |\\n| game scale: an iso camera (yaw 45°, pitch 42°) at about 19 and 22 pixels a metre, one art pixel per pixel, ×4 | Does it read in a game? (The engine's iso camera at zoom 1 shows about 20.) |\\n| palette, check | Which slots, which colours, which are unused; the problems and warnings, each naming the part and the fix |\\n\\nSheets are drawn in software (three shading bands, outlines) without point lights or looks: the\\ngame is the truth.\\n\\n## 2. Models\\n\\n```js\\nexport const crate = model({\\n  id: 'crate',\\n  about: 'Wooden crate with a frame and diagonal braces', // one line: find() searches it\\n  tags: ['container', 'wood'],\\n  palette: { wood: 'orange', band: 'red' }, // slots → the 16 palette names (GUIDE.md section 4)\\n  parts: [\\n    { box: [0.68, 0.68, 0.68], at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'wood' },\\n    boxFrame({ size: [0.72, 0.72, 0.72], beam: 0.09, at: [0, 0.36, 0], c: 'band' }),\\n    { mirror: [{ box: [0.8, 0.08, 0.04], at: [0, 0.36, 0.35], rot: [0, 0, 45], c: 'band' }], axis: 'z' },\\n  ],\\n});\\n```\\n\\nMetres and degrees; +Y is up, +Z is the front, the floor is y = 0. Parts are keyed, never\\npositional. **Every primitive fills its box `[w, h, d]`, centred on `at`**, turned by `rot` (XYZ,\\nabout `at`), and round kinds stretch to it:\\n\\n- `box`; `cyl` (an n-sided prism, `seg` sides, default 6 to 10 by size; `top`: the top width ÷\\n  the bottom width); `cone` (point up); `ball` (`half`: the upper dome); `lump` (a rock, `seed`);\\n  `wedge` (a ramp, full height at the back, −Z); `torus` (a ring in XZ as thick as the box is tall).\\n- Profile kinds use their own coordinates, placed at `at`: `lathe` ((radius, height) points\\n  turned about Y), `slab` (an XY outline or SVG path, extruded `depth` along Z), `tube` (points\\n  and a radius `r`, or a pair that tapers).\\n- Every part takes `c` (its slot; default the first), `name`, `glow: true` (unlit: a flame's\\n  heart, eyes), `tex` (a texture id, section 3), `seg` and `collide: false` (no box of its own\\n  under `collider: 'parts'`: a doorway's mortar ring).\\n\\n**Composition:**\\n\",\"total_lines\":283}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b36c83f6-330c-48d8-81d3-a7389435c6e5-23","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,49,54,48,55,95,95,120,54,57,56,49,101,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,48,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,49,54,48,55,95,95,120,54,57,56,49,