: readonly string[]): boolean;\\n    /** True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame). */\\n    wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean;\\n    /**\\n     * True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than\\n     * `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or\\n     * several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a\\n     * pause, a loading screen or a long hitch) never fires late.\\n     */\\n    consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean;\\n    /** `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array. */\\n    consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean;\\n    /** Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames. */\\n    state(): InputState;\\n    /** Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go. */\\n    setState(s: InputState, now: number): void;\\n    /** Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests). */\\n    get queuedPresses(): string[];\\n    /** Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input). */\\n    clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void;\\n    /**\\n     * -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left\\n     * stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\\n     */\\n    moveAxis(): {\\n        x: number;\\n        y: number;\\n    };\\n    /** Call at the end of each rendered frame. */\\n    endFrame(): void;\\n    /** Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false). */\\n    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not. */\\n    setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would. */\\n    setButtons(bits: number): void;\\n    /** Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`. */\\n    get mouseButtons(): number;\\n    /** A pointer button (a key code) goes down (pressed, unless held already) or up, where the pointer is. */\\n    private button;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer\\n     * was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where\\n     * it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play reads `isDown`.\\n     */\\n    get buttonEvents(): readonly ButtonEvent[];\\n    /**\\n     * Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked,\\n     * a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It\\n     * arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keeps it there, so\\n     * a replay delivers the same data at the same frame (and ignores what is sent live). It goes\\n     * through JSON first: live play reads exactly what a replay will.\\n     */\\n    send(command: unknown): void;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered\\n     * frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\\n     */\\n    get commands(): readonly unknown[];\\n\",\"total_lines\":278}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"79de2f58-6d82-4cf4-ad36-e41f180c3b77"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The getter for `audio.volume.music` has been identified.\n\nMusic volume will be set to 0.10 at boot from our own settings. This overwrites any persisted value and may affect other agents on the same file:// origin, but the plan requires it.\n\nThe first test run will see 0.10 because setup runs before the test reads the value. The engine settings panel might call setVolume at boot, but our setup in the first scene's setup will still enforce the correct volume. Each browser test launches fresh, so prior localStorage changes cannot leak into the next run.\n\nA distinct localStorage key will isolate our settings from the engine's shared `pixel-engine:audio` key. The AudioSettings interface needs review before writing begins.\n\nA pure `runActivity` function will be added to `src/core/play.js`. It runs the full activity simulation without the engine and returns stars, score, drips, seconds, and errors. The scene will call the same step functions during each fixed update so the shipped path is unified. Tests will cover 40 seeds of dot bots in a pure test file and activities via the headless scene or `runActivity`.\n\nThe plan forbids mocking the unit under test. Running all activities in one headless scene would force repeated hero setup, so a shared simulation module imported by both the scene and tests is preferable.\n\nTests will instantiate the activity runner class and step it directly. A headless scene integration test will also boot ActivityScene for a brief pose to verify wiring. The adversarial verifier requires tests to import the exact simulation functions that the scene uses, so the scene must call them.\n\nA headless smoke test will run the scene with an expert driver to completion. The scene is a thin wrapper around the scoring logic, so tests can exercise that core class directly. Visuals will be kept lightweight for fast headless runs.\n\nThe verification plan explicitly requires driving activity scenes from their real start states. Each activity will be tested by creating a fresh app, setting the driver skill, and running it headlessly through `Engine.headless` until completion.\n\nHeadless activity testing is estimated at 30 to 60 seconds total. The full suite including browser tests will be longer. The week playthrough will drive menus with a deterministic keyboard script. Flow states will use a single World scene that switches modes.\n\nThe single-player flow will run inside one persistent Game instance. This headless engine will handle menu navigation and setKey input for the entire playthrough. Activities will execute within this same engine. The World scene will initialize a simple ground and sky environment, then lazily spawn the hero and ounce.\n\nThe World scene delegates all logic to an App instance. Scenes are merely modes, and mode code will live in src/scenes/*.js files imported by App.\n\nThe week playthrough script is now defined: press Enter from title to main menu, select \"The Week\", choose slot 1 with three guests, confirm the default name, skip ferry, and auto-complete weigh-in.\n\nThe island will use a station list as the primary UI when standing in the plaza. Walking physics will remain available, but the \"Where today?\" board will open with stations as menu rows.\n\nThe island will use a ring-shaped station list with Left and Right to cycle the nearest stations. Confirming a choice tweens the hero automatically to that station and enters it. A mouse click on a station performs the same action. This controller-friendly interaction is playable and testable. The week playthrough script will press Down until \"Pavilion\" and Enter to reach the activity menu.\n\nThe week playthrough script will proceed to the \"Apologetic Tree\" activity, let the expert bot run it to completion, and then advance through the results screen. The driver skill will be set to expert so activities finish with stars. A 3x dev time scale was considered to speed things up, but the total game time would still be roughly 200 seconds.\n\nThe scripted week playthrough will skip the physics hero during activities to keep frame counts low. The hero can idle on the island between stations. PlatformerCharacter remains for real player movement. A Confession test is next.\n\nDay 7 will run The Long Stand prescription. After that the confession activity plays and chart points drop by seven. Credits follow and Week 2 begins with the next morning's weigh-in.\n\nThe test script will finish days one through six with at least one activity each and a prescription when possible. Day seven will perform the long stand. It will assert that ages decrease by seven, the week advances to two, and no lose state occurs.\n\nThe unlock schedule shows four activities open on day one: Apologetic Tree, Startled Heron, Squats of Regret, and Hoop Economics.\n\nThe unlock schedule continues through day six. Day two opens Coconut Header, Apology Lunges, Reluctant Warrior, and Locker Room. Day three adds Step Class, Slackline, and The Chair Nobody Asked For. Day four brings Vitamin Tray, Overhead Mortgage, and Tax Season. Day five reveals Island Jog, Pier Jump, and Plank Negotiation. Day six holds the hard versions. Drop-in is available from the start.\n\nJar milestones unlock locker items at ten, twenty-five, forty-five, seventy, one hundred, one hundred thirty-five, and one hundred seventy-five drips. A single expert 60-second activity yields about eighteen drips. Tests will verify that playing enough activities crosses these thresholds.\n\nThe jar milestone test will verify the first unlock at ten drips, since the full chain may not be reached. Save tests will check round-trip serialization, corruption resilience, and memory-only storage. A pause test is also being drafted.\n\nA pause test will step a stillness activity for sixty frames, record the dot and clock, then pause and resume. It will assert that pausing freezes time and resuming resumes normal advancement.\n\nDev sliders will be tested by setting each tunable to a different value, running a short step, comparing metrics to the default run, resetting the tab, and repeating. Some sliders may not affect a 0.1-second dot step.\n\nTunable values will be chosen so they visibly alter Dot.step outcomes, such as dotInstability 0.6 versus 5.0. The test will step a Dot with a fixed seed, change one tunable, and confirm the results differ. It will also verify that resetting the tab restores the default value. A live scene test will check that slider changes during an activity alter the scene fingerprint.\n\nAttract mode will be tested by stepping the title scene for over ten seconds without input and asserting the mode stays attract. Menu idle and activity idle will also remain in attract mode. Any key press exits to the title.\n\nThe attract playlist will run real scenes with expert bots and cheats. One full loop spans ten activities at fourteen seconds each, totaling roughly eight thousand frames.\n\nMedley testing will verify four bot lanes score correctly within their windows while the ghost lane never simulates. The event structure begins with a fifty-five-second still segment followed by a ten-second gap.\n\nThe medley event structure now includes a fifty-five-second beat segment and a fifty-five-second header segment, separated by ten-second gaps. Testing will use these real durations, but the simulation stays cheap because each lane is just a pure dot step rather than full 3D physics. The ghost lane will be verified by snapshot lerping with `simulated: false`, ensuring its velocity stays zero and its position matches the interpolated target instead of a physics step. Voice syllabification will be pure and deterministic, so identical words always produce the same syllable count.\n\nVoice rendering will use OfflineAudioContext in the browser test and rely on RMS detection there, while Node tests will only verify syllabification. The songs level check requires a volume sum of at least 0.375, so tracks will be set to 0.5–0.7 each. Clip validation will use the plan’s verified poses plus a few carefully added ones.\n\nNew clips will be authored in the verified style and validated; if validation is strict, only verified poses plus simple holds will be used. Model and texture checks will assert zero problems, fixing warnings only when trivial. A glyph test is also being drafted.\n\nA glyph test will collect every player-facing string and confirm each character belongs to the allowed set. A Nintendo names test will scan those strings for Wii, Mii, Balance Board, and related terms. The source field and filename are exceptions; about.js may reference Wii Fit, but player-facing world text must not.\n\nThe title constant will be verified in src/data/title.js and checked for hardcoded copies elsewhere. The exit-interview shape must match the build script output from about.js. PROGRESS.md will be written incrementally and finalized at the end. Playwright 1.63.0 needs to be installed early. pixel-engine.js must not be edited.\n\nThe pixel-engine.js hash will be compared against vendor/pixel-engine.sha256. All five browser flags are required. Touch detection will use a matchMedia query for coarse pointers without hover and no gamepad; the test passes touch: false to keep engine.touch falsy. WebGPU failure will be caught as an Engine.start rejection.\n\nThe WebGPU failure test will catch the Engine.start rejection, call AIHub.fail with the error message, and suppress rethrowing so no uncaught page error occurs. Family hour lanes will read navigator.getGamepads()[index].\n\nFamily hour tests will use a stub with two pads and a keyboard as lane three. Independent movement of each input source will be verified. PadDriver must read navigator.getGamepads directly instead of using ctx.input.moveAxis(). Gamepad buttons will be remapped at boot and the deadzone set to 0.12. The state() method will expose device type, controller glyph set, prompt text, and focus.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d1be0089-e945-46fd-8d30-b4c6c9c1f3cd-38","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"__PIXEL_ENGINE__","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/pixel-engine.js","head_limit":5}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d1be0089-e945-46fd-8d30-b4c6c9c1f3cd-39","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/audio/AudioManager.d.ts","offset":1,"limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2336,"output_tokens":4513,"cache_read_input_tokens":107520,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"ddbf3d0d-27d6-416b-80be-4cd7e251f03c"}
