{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Engine.start sets window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__.\n\nI'll also set __GAME__. I'm preparing to write the implementation carefully and iterate with tests. I need to verify validateClip, analyzeClip, and model() required fields so they pass. I'm reviewing the song format example for tracks and patterns.\n\nBoth tracks must have the same step count. The beat clock will fall back to game time when audio is unavailable, using audio time only when possible. A 104 BPM step chart spans 32 bars and 128 beats.\n\nCharts are strings where each character represents one step: L for left foot, R for right foot, and . for rest. The full 74-second step class test is acceptable for verification. A \"last one\" gag will announce reps plus two.\n\nBreath timing will hold on \"In\" and release on \"Out\", with smoothed sync and expert tracking. The plan is to write core files first, then a minimal scene and test dot before expanding. Dot will use a seeded mulberry32 random function for stable pure tests.\n\nDot's physics will use a delayed state buffer sampled every step. Each update blends random noise with a PD controller against the delayed position and velocity, then clamps to the unit disc.\n\nThe \"idle\" skill is not a bot behavior; it means zero input and zero stars. The actual skills are sloppy, average, good, and expert.\n\nRing time measurement will ignore the first two seconds of a 35-second window. A dot is in the ring when its distance from center is below a radius. The measure function will average over 40 seeds.\n\nDot physics updates use dt equal to one-sixtieth of a second at 60 Hz.\n\nInstability pushes the dot away from the center. Positive instability weight makes movement runaway. Bots fight this instability.\n\nI will copy the plan's clips exactly and verify placeFeet and validateClip.\n\nPress and Step clips will be simplified if analyzeClip rejects them. Hero clips will use compileClips with holds from the plan, validated by validateClip. I will check analyzeClip's return shape before writing. A test script will run after the clips are written. The project title is Mint Dial.\n\nThe tagline is \"Keep the dot in the circle.\" The about.js structure must feed both the credits screen and the exit interview. It contains source information, testing feedback, engine feedback, prompt feedback, AI hub data, credits, a disclaimer, and a kit summary. Feedback answers will be filled after real testing and then rebuilt. Usage notes that the module is added on import.\n\nResearch relied on the plan, kit guide, hub readme, and director's spikes. No extensive Wii Fit research was done beyond the public-mechanics summary.\n\nThe genre is a sports, fitness, and balance minigame collection set in an isometric island with fixed cameras. The tech stack is Pixel Engine 0.46.0, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, and esbuild 0.28.2. It supports one to four players. Controls follow the specified line. Tags include pixel art, comedy, versus, arcade, 3D, and story. The series label is Wii Fit.\n\nThe HUD menu system will use a Menu class with rows and focus tracking. Navigation skips disabled entries. The world frame switches between title and menu modes. Confirm actions use Enter, Space, KeyE, PadCross, or mouse left click. Space is reserved for menu confirmation only. Escape pauses gameplay and returns to the menu.\n\nEscape will return to the previous menu level. From the top pause menu it will resume gameplay.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-af4e7ea8-8192-4801-9aff-d0da440323b3-41","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/input.d.ts","offset":1,"limit":90}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-af4e7ea8-8192-4801-9aff-d0da440323b3-42","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"interface InputState|held:|pointer:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/input.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1142,"output_tokens":2109,"cache_read_input_tokens":115072,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"4f144e1f-bca4-421e-ab3f-1b20c3c64b12"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-af4e7ea8-8192-4801-9aff-d0da440323b3-41","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\\n * KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \\\"standard\\\" layout:\\n * 0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\\n */\\nexport declare const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>;\\n/** The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects). */\\nexport interface GamepadLike {\\n    readonly connected?: boolean;\\n10→    readonly mapping?: string;\\n    readonly axes: readonly number[];\\n    readonly buttons: readonly {\\n        readonly pressed: boolean;\\n        readonly value?: number;\\n    }[];\\n}\\n/**\\n * Radial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\\n * direction is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\\n20→ */\\nexport declare function applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone?: number): {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n};\\n/** How long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time. */\\nexport declare const PRESS_WINDOW = 0.15;\\n/**\\n * What the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\\n * Optional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\\n30→ * from the frame before.\\n */\\nexport interface InputFrame {\\n    /** Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it). */\\n    dt: number;\\n    /** Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted. */\\n    held?: string[];\\n    /** Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held). */\\n    pressed?: string[];\\n    /** The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone). */\\n40→    analog?: [number, number];\\n    pad?: [number, number];\\n    /** Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame. */\\n    delta?: [number, number];\\n    wheel?: number;\\n    /** Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says). */\\n    pointer?: [number, number, number, number];\\n    /** Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes. */\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    /** Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes. */\\n50→    touch?: boolean;\\n    /** Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`). */\\n    clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][];\\n    /** Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage. */\\n    commands?: unknown[];\\n    /**\\n     * Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise\\n     * callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\\n     */\\n    sync?: true;\\n60→}\\n/** What input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer. */\\nexport interface InputState {\\n    readonly held: readonly string[];\\n    /** Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed]. */\\n    readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[];\\n    readonly pointer: readonly [number, number, number, number];\\n    readonly gamepad: boolean;\\n    /** Data sent (`send`) and not yet delivered: the next frame that runs delivers it. */\\n    readonly outbox: readonly unknown[];\\n70→}\\n/** What the frame before held, for `snapshot()` to leave out what did not change. */\\nexport interface SnapshotMemory {\\n    held: string;\\n    pointer: string;\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    touch?: boolean;\\n}\\n/**\\n * Mouse buttons as key codes (`isDown('MouseLeft')`, `consumePress('MouseRight')`), each with\\n80→ * its `PointerEvent.buttons` bit, by `PointerEvent.button` (0 left, 1 middle, 2 right).\\n */\\nexport declare const MOUSE_BUTTONS: readonly (readonly [code: string, bit: number])[];\\n/** A finger on the canvas, as a key: held while one is down (not a mouse button, so a tap is not a click). */\\nexport declare const TOUCH_KEY = \\\"Touch\\\";\\n/** A pointer button pressed or let go (`Input.buttonEvents`): its key, and where the pointer was (normalised device coordinates). */\\nexport interface ButtonEvent {\\n    readonly code: string;\\n    readonly down: boolean;\\n    readonly x: number;\\n90→    readonly y: number;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\\n * KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \\\"standard\\\" layout:\\n * 0 A · 1 B · 2 X · 3 Y · 4 LB · 5 RB · 6 LT · 7 RT · 8 Back · 9 Start · 12–15 d-pad.\\n */\\nexport declare const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>;\\n/** The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects). */\\nexport interface GamepadLike {\\n    readonly connected?: boolean;\\n10→    readonly mapping?: string;\\n    readonly axes: readonly number[];\\n    readonly buttons: readonly {\\n        readonly pressed: boolean;\\n        readonly value?: number;\\n    }[];\\n}\\n/**\\n * Radial deadzone with rescaling: inside `deadzone` reads 0, the rim reads 1, and the\\n * direction is kept (no square-gate snapping to the axes).\\n20→ */\\nexport declare function applyDeadzone(x: number, y: number, deadzone?: number): {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n};\\n/** How long a queued press stays consumable, in seconds of game time. */\\nexport declare const PRESS_WINDOW = 0.15;\\n/**\\n * What the game could read from input in one frame (a recorded frame, see src/engine/replay.ts).\\n * Optional fields are left out when empty or zero; `held` and `pointer` also when unchanged\\n30→ * from the frame before.\\n */\\nexport interface InputFrame {\\n    /** Real seconds this frame lasted (game time follows from it). */\\n    dt: number;\\n    /** Keys held, keyboard and gamepad buttons together, sorted. */\\n    held?: string[];\\n    /** Keys pressed since the frame before (a tap can be pressed and not held). */\\n    pressed?: string[];\\n    /** The touch joystick and the gamepad's left stick (after its deadzone). */\\n40→    analog?: [number, number];\\n    pad?: [number, number];\\n    /** Pointer movement (CSS pixels) and wheel ticks this frame. */\\n    delta?: [number, number];\\n    wheel?: number;\\n    /** Pointer position (normalised device coordinates), over the canvas (1/0), buttons (`mouseButtons`: what `held` says). */\\n    pointer?: [number, number, number, number];\\n    /** Whether a gamepad is connected (`gamepadConnected`), when that changes. */\\n    gamepad?: boolean;\\n    /** Whether the pointer is a touch (`pointer.touch`), when that changes. */\\n50→    touch?: boolean;\\n    /** Pointer buttons pressed (1) and let go (0) this frame, in order, each where the pointer was (`buttonEvents`). */\\n    clicks?: [string, 0 | 1, number, number][];\\n    /** Data from outside the canvas delivered this frame (`send()`): a DOM widget's value, a save read from storage. */\\n    commands?: unknown[];\\n    /**\\n     * Ran in the same task as the frame before (frames of one `step(n)` call): no promise\\n     * callbacks ran between them. A replay waits for them before every other frame.\\n     */\\n    sync?: true;\\n60→}\\n/** What input a later frame still reads, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): keys held, presses not yet used and how old they are, the pointer. */\\nexport interface InputState {\\n    readonly held: readonly string[];\\n    /** Queued presses: [key, seconds of game time since it was pressed]. */\\n    readonly queued: readonly (readonly [string, number])[];\\n    readonly po