2,70,105,116,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,49,54,48,55,47,116,121,112,101,115,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,97,110,105,109,97,116,105,111,110,47,112,111,115,101,46,100,46,116,115,10,50,49,58,101,120,112,111,114,116,32,100,101,99,108,97,114,101,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,118,97,108,105,100,97,116,101,67,108,105,112,40,99,108,105,112,58,32,67,108,105,112,68,101,102,44,32,114,105,103,58,32,82,105,103,83,112,101,99,41,58,32,115,116,114,105,110,103,91,93,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":2,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/animation/metrics.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":68,\"content\":\"export declare function analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport;\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/animation/pose.d.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":21,\"content\":\"export declare function validateClip(clip: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec): string[];\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"8dc4cedc-754a-4bbe-883e-f4f9455225ab"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_26","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"analyzeClip requires a model and a compiled clip.\n\nHERO_MODEL loads without an asset reader. I will build the hero and compile its clips in a test to validate analyzeClip.\n\nI will load the hero model once in a minimal test context. I am now drafting the scoring logic for stillness, which awards points only during the hold phase and weights ring presence against breath synchronization.\n\nBreath timing is now defined: a cycle period with an In phase and an Out phase. The score approaches 1 when the dot holds during In or releases during Out, and expert mode requires exact synchronization. Gentle mode scales ring size by 1.25 and reduces noise to 0.8, with a tunable ringScale. God mode clamps the dot inside the ring. Stillness bot targets will follow a slow ±0.12 bias wander on x so the expert tracks the shifting equilibrium point.\n\nThe bias wander will be implemented as a constant force m.bx rather than a shifting target offset. The dot’s equilibrium position is x = -(auth * u + bx) / inst, so the expert must counterbalance the bias to hold the dot at zero. The bias will vary as 0.12 * sin(0.3 * t).\n\nThe expert will target zero to counter the drift, keeping the dot inside the 0.36 ring. If the expert scores below two stars, I will tune the bot or bias strength. Activities must have idle zero and expert at least two stars; activity modifiers can be adjusted while the dot model stays exact.\n\nFor reps, each rep lasts announced plus two total reps. Tempo divides by repTempoScale. The phase breakdown is up 0.4, hold 0.15, down 0.35, and rest 0.10.\n\nRep input depth maps stick position to a 0..1 range. The expert drives the dot toward the negative target, with response lag modeled by exponential smoothing or a capped k-style filter. Quality is sampled only during active phases, not rest, and computed as one minus 1.6 times the mean absolute deviation from target, clamped to zero and one. Final score equals that mean times one hundred, with star thresholds at fifty-five, seventy-five, and ninety. Fatigue tremor scales with the square of the rep-to-total ratio.\n\nIdle quality is 0.28, yielding zero stars. The expert lags the target by about 0.1 seconds, producing a quality of roughly 0.875 and two stars. To reach three stars, the expert must lead the target by an amount equal to lag times the rate of change, clamped. This keeps error small and quality above 0.95.\n\nPress uses a repLag of 1.5 and still compensates for target lag. Idle is zero, expert is three, and sloppy is worse than expert.\n\nHoop scoring integrates angular velocity while the dot stays inside the speed band. The band direction alternates between anticlockwise and clockwise every twenty-five seconds, or either way for ten seconds. Angular velocity is unwrapped and smoothed over 0.25 seconds.\n\nThe expert spins the hoop at two rotations per second inside the 0.9–2.4 speed band. That yields 120 rotations over 60 seconds, which clears the 110-point threshold for three stars. Idle scores zero. The lift gauge starts at one so the expert can keep it up; a value of zero triggers the drop. Thrown hoops are optional bonuses.\n\nHoop score is the raw rotation count inside the band, not a scaled value. The expert may skip easy catches to preserve that count.\n\nStep accuracy is a weighted sum of perfect, good, and okay hits divided by total notes. The expert achieves all perfects for three stars; idle misses yield zero. Step windows scale with stepWindow.\n\nA chart pattern of N notes over 74 seconds will be generated so the expert can hit every one.\n\nStep notes are paired with foot presses and hit when nearest within the window. The header activity spawns objects every interval, lerping spawn rate from 1.3 to 0.7 over sixty seconds, with a 1.4-second fall time. Types are seventy percent coconut, twenty-five percent flip, and five percent pine, landing randomly in lanes. Coconut hits add ten points times combo tier, flips subtract ten and reset combo, and pines add thirty. Combo tiers double at five, triple at ten, and quadruple at twenty. The expert predicts the next landing x and moves there; speed is four point five, distance cap is four meters, arrival time is zero point eight nine seconds, and the telegraph is one point four seconds.\n\nThe expert will avoid flips by dodging and target head coconuts and pines. A score above 800 should yield three stars.\n\nIdle performance is expected to stay below 150 points. With sixty objects and a 0.32 hit radius, only about six coconuts land in range. That yields roughly forty points, which is too low to trigger combo multipliers.\n\nTo keep the test deterministic, I will use a fixed seed and assert results across several seeds. If variance remains high, I will shrink the hit radius to 0.22 so idle hits drop to about eleven percent.\n\nThe step hit radius will be 0.18 if the test still fails after multiple seeds, rather than biasing spawns away from center. Slackline uses a Dot model with instability scaled by walking speed and noise of 1.25, with y locked; the expert walks forward at 1.1 m/s when input exceeds 0.3, falls if horizontal position exceeds one meter, respawns at the nearest checkpoint, and receives a 1.2-second stun.\n\nSlackline gusts strike every six meters for one second with a ±1.6 bias and a 1.5-second telegraph. The expert walks only while horizontal position stays within 0.45 meters, using PD control to keep the dot near zero and counter gusts. The activity limits length to thirty meters and time to seventy-five seconds. Star thresholds are zero for unfinished runs, one for finishing, two for one fall under forty-five seconds, and three for zero falls under thirty-two seconds. Score equals 1000 minus sixty times falls minus eight times elapsed time over twenty-seven seconds. Idle mode yields zero stars because the expert never starts walking.\n\nSlackline should take roughly 28 to 30 seconds with no falls, yielding three stars.\n\nTray uses a kinematic model where vitamins fall under gravity and tilt. Tilt lags toward the input direction. The expert steers all vitamins toward their matching cups with one shared tilt.\n\nA funnel-shaped board with walls will guide each vitamin into its matching cup. The expert will use a fixed tilt sequence: +x for red, -z for blue, and so on. This scripted policy is simpler than a shared-tilt steering solution.\n\nThe tray will use an open arena with walls that do not block direct paths. The expert will tilt toward the vitamin nearest its cup, reducing distance sequentially. Eight vitamins can be solved in about sixty seconds at roughly 2.37 m/s² acceleration.\n\nTray time is about sixteen seconds. The expert finishes with three stars. Idle mode scores zero.\n\nWrong-cup penalties are avoided by placing cups far apart and vitamins closer to the correct cup. The greedy tilt policy still works.\n\nJog uses alternating steps in a 2.4–3.6 speed band. Eighteen cups along the path award points when the player is within 0.4 meters. Score equals seventy times pace plus thirty times cup percentage. Star thresholds are fifty-five, seventy-five, and ninety. The expert alternates at three hertz and steers toward cups for roughly one hundred points. Idle scores zero.\n\nJog speed is capped at 3.0 so the expert avoids the 3.6 band stitch.\n\nPier has three attempts. The run-up lerps toward 14 m/s if the dot lands in [-0.8, -0.4], otherwise toward 6 m/s. At five seconds the player must press within 0.25 seconds of the lip for a bonus. Flight lasts 3.5 seconds inside a 0.45 meter ring with instability 3. Distance score uses ring fraction. A cannonball press in the last half-second gives style points but does not affect star thresholds.\n\nThe expert will hold the dot in a tuck position during the run-up and flight, targeting y=-0.6 and x=0 at the lip. It will press exactly at the lip for the bonus and perform a cannonball. Speed builds linearly from 6 m/s to 14 m/s over the five-second run-up. Distance is computed as speed multiplied by (0.8+0.6p), (0.6+0.4*ringFrac), and pierBoost, which defaults to 2.14.\n\nPier distance at full speed and ring fraction yields roughly 42 meters, satisfying the three-star threshold. Idle mode scores near 7 meters, so it receives zero stars. The expert clears all three attempts while idle fails them all.\n\nLong stand is a ninety-second stillness challenge where timeline events modify ring size, bias, impulses, and noise. Score equals one hundred times the time spent inside the ring. Star thresholds are forty-five, seventy, and ninety.\n\nThe expert should stay above ninety percent inside the ring even when brief impulses strike. A y-0.6 impulse drops the ring to 0.24, but recovery keeps the score near ninety-eight percent. Idle mode scores only about eight percent and receives zero stars. If the expert falls below seventy percent, impulse magnitudes will be reduced while the events remain; god mode is not used for this test.\n\nAttract uses god mode. Drips calculates drops as two plus three times stars plus the play phase duration in thirty-second intervals, with an extra five for prescription mode. Tests will verify both the exported function and the explicit formula.\n\nSloppy skill must score below the expert on every activity. Easy activities will be tree, heron, squat, lunge, hoop, header, jog, and pier.\n\nHard versions will use 85 percent of the ring size and tempo, with a tighter hoop band. The expert must still score at least two stars on these harder variants.\n\nThe step activity will include a \"last one\" gag where the test asserts `result.lied === true` and the total reps equal the announced count plus two.\n\nA deterministic mulberry32 PRNG will be exported for reproducible randomization. The bot driver constants are now defined: idle is null, sloppy uses tau 0.42 and kp 0.7, average uses tau 0.30 and kp 1.1, good uses tau 0.22 and kp 1.5, and expert uses tau 0.17 and kp 1.9.\n\nThe BotDriver class implements the skill-based control logic. It stores a history of dot states and computes desired positions from that history using the configured skill's time constant.\n\nThe driver discards stale history entries older than the target time constant. It adds random noise scaled by the skill error and decays it slightly each frame. The control law computes a PD force from the dot’s last seen position and velocity against the current target, normalizes the resulting vector to unit magnitude, and returns it as the movement command.\n\nThe ring measurement test will run for thirty-five seconds with forty seeds, discarding the first two seconds and averaging the in-ring fraction. This will be executed first to verify the dot and bot before building the remaining activities. The engine uses a fixed timestep of one-sixtieth of a second.\n\nThe local driver will use keyboard smoothing: a 0.10-second attack and 0.06-second release for the attack button, with shift half-lean. Mouse input maps to a force vector from the dial center offset and clamped to the disc radius. The driver will be written to accept raw input state so it can be tested without the engine. Playwright will be installed in the background. Kit credits will be imported at build time for the about screen.\n\nThe about screen will display a shortened credits summary. The test checks for the string \"This is not medical advice. Ounce is a scale.\" and source fields. Embedding the full CREDITS.md file at runtime is impossible because fetch is unavailable on file:// URLs.\n\nThe full CREDITS.md will be embedded in the browser bundle as a generated module. A build step will read the file and output a JavaScript string. This avoids runtime fetch on file:// URLs.\n\nThe build will use esbuild with a `.md=text` loader so the game can import `../../CREDITS.md` directly. The pixel-engine ES module is 4.8 MB, and the bundled HTML will be about 5 MB. Node tests will import the engine source directly via ES modules because `package.json` sets `\"type\": \"module\"`, avoiding duplicate bundling.\n\nHeadless mode is confirmed. Tests run from the dev folder via `node tools/test.mjs`. HeadlessOptions defaults to a 960×540 viewport, and widget tests will use browser page viewports. The world scene must call the HUD so widgets exist; in headless mode the HUD constructor accepts a null container, so text calls may no-op but width should still be set.\n\nThe implementation will proceed in layers: core engine first, then the dot test, simulations, the game shell, and finally the browser build. PROGRESS.md will be updated as each layer is completed.\n\nA Nintendo name scan revealed that \"Wii Fit\" is allowed because it appears only in the exit interview's source field and filename. Player-facing text tests will not check those fields.\n\nThe voice syllabification design uses a hash-based algorithm to split words into 2-3 letter chunks. It defines a set of consonants and five vowel formants with specific frequencies, then hashes the word to determine chunk boundaries.\n\nThe syllabification code strips non-alphabetic characters and splits each word into two-letter chunks, leaving the final chunk as one letter if the word length is odd.\n\nThe syllabification hash now combines the word with its index so each syllable is stable yet distinct. The voice engine will use fixed speaker parameters: Ounce at 340 Hz and 13 rate, Coach at 165 Hz and 7.5 rate with flat pitch. Pitch will decline by fifteen percent across the sentence, rise twenty-five percent at the end, and slow down.\n\nThe voice engine will output a sequence of syllable objects containing timing, duration, pitch, and phonetic parameters. A question mark raises the final syllable's pitch by 25 percent relative to its normal value. Tests will verify that identical words always produce the same syllables and that question sentences end with a higher pitch than non-question versions. The browser offline test will validate rendered audio by checking RMS amplitude exceeds 0.02 and Ounce pitch is higher than Coach pitch, using autocorrelation or zero-crossing pitch detection on the buffer.\n\nThe voice render will use a 6 ms attack and 30 ms decay envelope to keep RMS amplitude near 0.1, satisfying the 0.02 threshold. Songs will be mixed at 0.55 and 0.5 so their combined volume meets the 0.375 minimum. parseSong must succeed and each track must have matching step counts. The step song will run at 104 BPM. The long stand song will start near silence and then swell.\n\nThe level check sums track volumes rather than note density, so sparse instrument patterns remain audible. The full SFX register list is now defined. The browser boot test will launch the root HTML twice from file:// and verify zero page errors, Mint Dial title screen state names, and music volume at 0.10.\n\nThe HTML output will be placed in the workspace root, not the dev folder. Playwright will run Chromium with five flags. The capture test will render frame 30, composite the HUD, and save a PNG. It will verify the buffer is not blank and check pixel variance. If capture throws, it will log the error and fall back to state assertions only. Both browser runs must agree on title, music volume, and mode.\n\nThe phone profile test will use Playwright’s iPhone 13 device preset. It will verify touch message text, the presence of a settings button via the `settings-button` CSS class, and confirm that touch gameplay is disabled. The attract screen will be detected by waiting ten seconds or by fast-forwarding the engine with `e.step(700)`.\n\nWebkit and Firefox lack WebGPU, so the engine must catch its startup rejection to avoid a page error. A fatal div may also be set, but the promise rejection should be handled first.\n\nThe mode join test will use two stub pads and a keyboard to press cross, start the medley, set different axes, and verify lane x positions differ. A `getGamepads` stub must be injected into the page so the PadDriver can read navigator state. For audio verification, an analyser node will be connected to the audio context destination after click to confirm non-silent RMS levels from music and SFX.\n\nThe test cannot directly tap the engine's audio bus. It will create an AnalyserNode on the audio context and connect it to the destination node, then feed a test source into that same destination so the analyser reads non-silent output.\n\nTapping the engine's destination node directly is impossible because AudioDestinationNode has no outputs to connect. The engine's gain node might be accessible, but inspecting minified audio manager internals is impractical.\n\nThe engine may have already created its AudioContext before the test can wrap it. I will inspect runtime properties after boot to see if the audio buses are accessible. The TypeScript `private` keyword might compile to a regular property or to a true private field, so I need to verify at runtime.\n\nThe engine exports `renderSound`, so the test can generate its own audio for verification. The analyser will be connected to whatever gain node is exposed on the audio object after boot, rather than tapping the destination directly.\n\nThe widget overflow test will collect widgets at three viewport sizes and assert they fit within the HUD width and height. The hub test will copy its HTML, harness, bridge, and round files into a temporary folder inside the dev directory, using the scratch directory only for logs.\n\nThe hub test files will be placed in `dev/vendor/hub-run/` rather than the scratch directory. This keeps them inside the work folder while avoiding a large HTML commit.\n\nA local Python HTTP server will serve the hub test files from a temporary folder under the dev directory, then terminate after Playwright finishes. Playwright will open four URLs and wait for dataset completion and hub pass signals. A no-harness test will verify the game boots without the harness and that AIHub is inactive. The harness will also confirm the document title is Mint Dial before trusting the page.\n\nThe harness play window is only about six seconds, so the medley must not finish naturally. The test will call ready() within twenty seconds without input, answer ready on Enter, begin sending progress and ghost sends immediately at score zero, and call result() on time-up.\n\nThe harness will call `AIHub.ready()` immediately when the card loads, without waiting for a key press. The Enter key will then trigger `AIHub.answer('ready')` as the player's response. Both signals are required before the harness considers the round ready.\n\nA direct window keydown listener will handle Enter and M or Triangle presses, since the engine may not hold focus. The countdown lasts three seconds before AIHub.time() reaches zero; gameplay begins once time() becomes non-negative. During the short harness round, progress is checked only after the phase advances to playing, with random input applied for 1.5 seconds.\n\nProgress must be sent immediately when the start signal arrives, even during the countdown and at score zero. The leave action should let the avatar wave and walk off without crashing. At end of round, input and sound stop and nothing is sent. If connect returns null, the normal game flow continues. WebGPU startup failures will be caught by the engine and reported to AIHub via a fail message.\n\nThe boot sequence will call `AIHub.ready()` immediately after `Engine.start()` resolves, before heavy setup finishes. This prevents a late ready signal if the connection is slow.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8ed670fa-1df2-43ac-b9b9-574095ec41d9-48","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"seed\\?:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/Engine.d.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8ed670fa-1df2-43ac-b9b9-574095ec41d9-49","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/CREDITS.md","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":825,"output_tokens":9586,"cache_read_input_tokens":123392,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"33ea78e1-2fc4-4534-ae5a-16dcc3da076d"}
