rt 100.0%\\nring 0.3 idle 7.0%  sloppy 12.6%  average 50.4%  good 92.4%  expert 100.0%\\nring 0.22 idle 5.4%  sloppy 6.2%  average 29.5%  good 72.0%  expert 97.9%\\nring 0.16 idle 1.6%  sloppy 3.6%  average 19.5%  good 47.7%  expert 77.5%\\n(node:3873569) Warning: The 'NO_COLOR' env is ignored due to the 'FORCE_COLOR' env being set.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"cd \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607\\\" && node --input-type=module -e '\\nimport { Dot, DOT_DEFAULTS } from \\\"./src/core/dot.js\\\";\\nimport { BotDriver } from \\\"./src/core/drivers.js\\\";\\nimport { mulberry32 } from \\\"./src/core/rng.js\\\";\\n\\nconst dt = 1/60;\\nconst T = { ...DOT_DEFAULTS };\\nfunction frac(skill, ring, seed) {\\n  const rand = mulberry32(seed);\\n  const dot = new Dot(rand);\\n  const bot = new BotDriver(skill, mulberry32(seed + 99), () => ({x:0,y:0}));\\n  let inn = 0, tot = 0, t = 0;\\n  const frames = Math.round(35 / dt);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < frames; i++) {\\n    const u = skill === \\\"idle\\\" ? {x:0,y:0} : bot.read(dot, dt);\\n    dot.step(dt, u.x, u.y, T, {});\\n    t += dt;\\n    if (t > 2) { tot++; if (Math.hypot(dot.x, dot.y) < ring) inn++; }\\n  }\\n  return inn / tot;\\n}\\nconst skills = [\\\"idle\\\",\\\"sloppy\\\",\\\"average\\\",\\\"good\\\",\\\"expert\\\"];\\nconst rings = [0.40, 0.30, 0.22, 0.16];\\nfor (const ring of rings) {\\n  const row = [];\\n  for (const skill of skills) {\\n    let s = 0;\\n    for (let seed = 1; seed <= 40; seed++) s += frac(skill, ring, seed * 1000 + Math.round(ring*100));\\n    row.push(skill + \\\" \\\" + (100 * s / 40).toFixed(1) + \\\"%\\\");\\n  }\\n  console.log(\\\"ring\\\", ring, row.join(\\\"  \\\"));\\n}\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Measure Dot time-in-ring for bot skills\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3%2Fwork/01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469/terminal/call-a65bc8c6-aa3e-417d-b6aa-02da99c07c6d-60.log\",\"total_bytes\":505}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"01d8332e-2ea2-4e0b-b4a5-c0eb9cd3ee81"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_31","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bands match the plan's table very closely.\n\nThe dot model is confirmed correct. The remaining files to write are voice.js, songs.js, sfx.js, font.js, activities data, sims.js, art assets, app.js, world.js, main.js, tests, and build.\n\nDesigning the activity simulation system. Activity definitions will live in activities.js with metadata and thresholds, while sim logic goes in sims.js. A dispatcher function will route each activity kind to its specific simulator. A shared PhaseSim class will handle common phase machine behavior across all simulations.\n\nPhaseSim tracks phase timing and state. It defines a five-phase sequence: intro, practice, countdown, play, and outro. Practice mode runs the play logic without scoring.\n\nPractice mode will run the full mechanic for eight seconds without accumulating score. Tests should include this unscored phase to match the real menu flow. The star calculation remains unchanged because scoring only happens during the play phase.\n\nStillness play length is four seconds settle, the hold duration, and two seconds release. Scoring only counts during the hold phase.\n\nStillSim.step advances the phase machine, reads the pose and ring, applies modifiers, steps the dot model, and clamps the ring if god mode is active.\n\nStillSim scoring now counts frames only during the hold window after the four-second settle. It records dot position relative to the ring and samples breath input when the pose requires it. A clean phase advancer is still needed. The breathing logic uses a periodic half-cycle to distinguish inhale from exhale.\n\nBreath scoring now uses a smoothed target value driven by the correct inhale or exhale state. The noise modifier scales with a tunable breathBonus, defaulting to 0.55 to match the plan. Final score weights ring accuracy at eighty percent and breath consistency at twenty percent when breathing is required. A bias wander effect for the heron dot is also being drafted.\n\nThe heron dot will use a small periodic force for bias wander. Chair noise will ramp from 1 to 1.5 during the hold. Fold bias is ±0.18. Plank intensity scales by 1.2 and gains tremor after twenty seconds.\n\nExpert bot targets are set. Stillness aims at the center. Heron targets the ring at 0.36 for three stars. Plank targets the ring at 0.24 with a chance of tremor dropping the score.\n\nPlank noise will increase slightly to 1.3 after twenty seconds. Hard plank targets are now set: 0.22 ring yields ninety-eight percent accuracy, and 0.20 ring yields about ninety percent, satisfying the two-star requirement.\n\nA dedicated RepSim is being designed for repetition activities. It will use a dot for visual feedback but score independently of the dot position.\n\nRepSim will lead its target by a fixed lag. The expert bot will look ahead using the derivative of the target curve, then clamp that lead position to the valid range.\n\nThe expert bot tracks the target using a low-pass filter with a fixed lag. The lead offset equals the target slope multiplied by that lag. Depth is clamped to the valid range. For squat, the local player derives depth from the stick axis.\n\nPress depth maps to the stick up axis. Lunge depth alternates left and right on the stick. The scene will call a new `autoInput` helper from sims.js to fetch expert inputs for active skills, falling back to local player input when idle.\n\nWorld fixedUpdate will run the same autoInput path so headless play uses the expert bots. RepSim quality is computed by averaging absolute error during the up, hold, and down phases, then clamping `1 - 1.6 * mean` to a one-hundred-point scale. Fatigue adds a small sine-driven offset scaled by the square of the rep ratio; this can push quality from ninety-five percent down to zero.\n\nFatigue is pushing expert scores just below the three-star threshold. A twelve-rep set can drop quality from ninety-five percent to about eighty-nine percent, which is too close to miss three stars. The expert lead will be adjusted so fatigue acts as a zero-mean tremor rather than a systematic drift, and the amplitude scaling is being reduced to keep easy squats reliably at three stars.\n\nThe expert lead will be tuned so the systematic error stays near zero. Fatigue will act purely as a zero-mean tremor, adding only about two percent of extra tracking error. This keeps easy squats reliably at three stars. Sloppy reps will lag behind and overshoot to score lower.\n\nIdle rep quality is calculated as roughly seven points, confirming zero stars. The coach gag is being implemented with flags that trigger \"Last one.\" and \"I lied.\" at the final announced rep.\n\nThe coach gag triggers at the final announced rep, repeats \"Last one.\" on the next, and says \"I lied. This is the last one.\" on the third. The hoop simulator tracks angular velocity with a smoothed derivative of the stick input, reverses direction after twenty-five and fifty seconds, and scores points only while the spin rate stays inside the target band.\n\nThe hoop expert spins at two rotations per second in the correct direction for each band. This yields one hundred and ten total rotations, satisfying the three-star requirement. Idle input produces no rotation, so score stays at zero.\n\nSloppy reps will spin at 0.4 rev/s, below the target band, to score low.\n\nThrown hoops appear every nine seconds with a one-and-a-half-second telegraph and a 0.4-second catch window. The expert can keep circling and may accidentally catch; catches add bonus rotations but are not required for three stars.\n\nThe header, step notes from the chart, and hit detection are being implemented.\n\nThe slackline simulator will use a dot with modifiers, track progress, and handle falls.\n\nStar thresholds are being replaced by a special-cased scoring rule: three stars for zero falls under thirty-two seconds, two stars for one fall under forty-five seconds, and one star otherwise. The score formula will match this logic.\n\nThe remaining mechanics are now mapped out. Tray will be a kinematic board. Jog will track cadence and cups. Pier will allow three attempts. Long stand will use a timeline. Weigh-in will get its own simulator factory.\n\nEach skill will expose an `auto()` method so the scene can call it directly. Hard mode will shrink the ring to eighty-five percent of its default size and scale it by the ring scale factor, then apply a gentle multiplier of 1.25.\n\nHard slackline will use a 1.15 multiplier on walk speed and a shorter time limit, with slightly stronger gusts and noise. The expert should still finish under forty-five seconds with at most one fall for two stars.\n\nHard header will use a seventy-five percent faster interval and a smaller hit window. The expert will still track, keeping the score above four hundred points, so three stars are not required there.\n\nEasy skills that can reliably earn three stars include tree, heron, squat, lunge, hoop, step, header, jog, pier, slackline, and possibly warrior.\n\nI will assert three stars for tree, heron, squat, hoop, step, header, and jog, and two stars for all other skills including hard mode. I am now drafting the coach dialogue as a compact data file with one hundred thirty-six short sentences under fifty-two characters each, then checking them against the test character limit.\n\nVoice, songs, and font are being written in parallel with the simulators. Songs must stay short but valid, with combined volumes at least 0.375. The step song will run at 104 BPM. A `loop` helper will build note strings for both tracks, and a `bar` function will verify that each track has an equal number of steps.\n\nVoice rendering will use a browser OfflineAudioContext with sawtooth oscillators. Each syllable carries f0, vowel, consonant, duration, and timing data. The render function accepts the audio context, destination node, syllables array, and optional start time.\n\nThe voice synthesizer now uses two bandpass filters per syllable, driven by sawtooth oscillators. Each syllable’s gain envelope starts at zero, rises to 0.4 over six milliseconds, then fades to near silence after the longer of thirty-six milliseconds or the syllable duration. The oscillator stops slightly past the end of the syllable plus a small tail. A noise burst will simulate plosive consonants. The default f0 is 340 Hz for the ounce speaker and 165 Hz for the coach.\n\nThe voice synthesizer gain is being raised to 0.8 and the filter Q lowered to 2 so the RMS stays above 0.02. The syllabify function splits text into words, flattens them into syllables with character indices, and computes durations from the speaker rate.\n\nThe syllabify function now infers speech rate from punctuation: slow text runs at three-quarters speed. Pitch falls by fifteen percent across the phrase, or stays flat for a speaker like the coach. Final-syllable pitch is raised by twenty-five percent for questions, fifteen percent for exclamations, and twelve percent for Dennis.\n\nConsonant and vowel choices for each syllable are now hashed from the word plus its index within that word, not the sentence. The test will verify that identical words like \"hello\" produce the same phonetic sequence across different sentences. For questions, the final syllable pitch must be exactly 1.25 times the pitch of the same final syllable in a matching statement.\n\nThe coach will use a flat pitch with no declination, while the ounce speaker retains pitch variation. Each syllable will carry a character index pointing to its original text so the typewriter can stay synchronized. I am now writing all files, starting with the simulators in one large batch.\n\ndevice.js and actions.js will be kept small. The main app.js is being structured as an App class with a World wrapper that delegates fixedUpdate, update, status, and fingerprint to the core game loop.\n\nThe World class now delegates cameraTarget to the core app. The mount method wires the engine, input remap, volume, and global game state. bootWorld builds the ground, props, and characters, then sets the initial mode to title or card. If a session exists, the card mode triggers the medley on the AIHub start event. The ready callback runs after Engine.start, so setup must finish quickly; loading the embedded hero model is acceptable as long as it stays under twenty seconds.\n\nThe card scene will use simple colored boxes as avatars instead of heavy hero models, since setup must finish before the ready callback. The normal game path can still load the full hero model during setup. A conditional branch in setup will build the card for sessions and the island art otherwise.\n\nThe normal game will use a simple island built from boxes plus the ounce model, with the hero loaded afterward and box avatars as fallback. I will write the models now and test them with a tiny headless game to catch checkModel errors like negative part sizes or missing palette slots. The clips will be copied directly from the plan.\n\nI will import `placeFeet`, `mirror`, and `HERO_RIG` into clips.js. The test will load the hero model to validate clips that need a skeleton. I am copying the plan’s `hold()` and `feet()` helpers, which Warrior, Chair, and Fold use but Tree and Heron do not. The jog simulator may include a gait clip for visual effect even though it does not affect scoring.\n\nThe jog simulator will include a gait clip if validation passes. The header skill interval is now tunable via `headerInterval`, defaulting to 1.0 so the base spawn timing stays unchanged. Play seconds will be measured as elapsed time during the scored play phase, and drip effects will use `floor(playSeconds / 30)`.\n\nDrip counts are now deterministic because the simulator records the exact play duration in result.seconds. The app adds a prescription bonus of five points when the goal is met, but the activity result stores only the base drip count.\n\nDrip counts will be computed entirely by the app from stars and seconds, with the simulator returning only those values. The results screen will display app-computed drips, and the test will read them from state().lastDrips. The app will also store lastResult. Simulators will use a BotDriver for auto input during stillness.\n\nThe auto-input logic now returns zero for idle skills and otherwise queries the BotDriver at sixty-hertz intervals. A breathing signal is generated from the activity period. The driver’s internal clock advances only once per step, so it stays synchronized with the scene’s dot movement.\n\nThe engine fixedUpdate interval is locked to 1/60 seconds. The scene passes this delta into each simulator’s auto method. Skills are stored on the simulator and can be swapped mid-run by updating the options and replacing the BotDriver. Idle mode returns zero inputs. Hoop mode ignores the dot bot and circles instead. Header mode steers horizontally while keeping vertical input at zero. Reps mode sets depth and also applies horizontal and vertical inputs for show.\n\nRep auto-input now varies by skill level. Idle keeps depth at zero. Expert leads the target. Sloppy applies a delayed noisy target. Average uses a partial lead with noise. Good also leads with noise.\n\nSloppy depth is scaled to thirty percent of the target. Quality stays low.\n\nStep auto presses when the note time is within half a step of the play time. Sloppy hits land one hundred twenty milliseconds late. That falls inside the one hundred forty millisecond window. The sloppy score may reach forty points. That is below the fifty-five point threshold for stars. Expert scores one hundred. The test expects sloppy to score lower than expert.\n\nStep timing windows scale with a configurable stepWindow multiplier, while stepOffsetMs shifts the judged time by default zero. Confession is handled outside the simulator; the app plays the ending after longstand results are confirmed. Long stand events will be stored as data inside the simulator. I am now writing the WeighSim class.\n\nThe WeighSim constructor initializes a Dot, a BotDriver, and state variables for timing, samples, hold duration, and target hits. It defines three lean targets at (-0.7, 0), (0.7, 0), and (0, 0.6). The target method returns the center point for stillness or the current indexed lean target.\n\nThe chase target traces a lissajous curve. The step method advances time and updates the dot. Stillness samples are recorded between three and fourteen seconds when the dot stays within a radius of zero point three. Lean targets trigger hold accumulation only when the dot is within a radius of zero point one.\n\nThe lean path advances to the next target after a one-point-five-second hold. The chase path records distance samples. After fourteen seconds the activity finishes. Stillness scores from average sample distance. Lean scores from hit ratio. Chase scores from normalized distance.\n\nExpert stillness scores near one after two seconds, but sampling begins at three seconds. Idle stillness stays near zero. Expert lean reaches all targets in about ten and a half seconds, while idle lean never holds. Chase expert tracks the moving target with some lag. Age maps to roughly twenty for expert and eighty for idle.\n\nThe app will run the WeighSim, then pause for a question, reveal the age, and confirm before moving to the island. The test can press confirm through both the question and reveal screens. The feel age picker is shown once before the first weigh-in and is not saved.\n\nA `runSim` helper will let tests drive simulators directly or through the engine. The engine path will use World to run an activity until results with step(). One activity will be verified first, then all. FixedUpdate dt will be logged to check for engine issues. At normal speed one headless frame likely triggers one fixed update.\n\nSimulators will use the dt argument directly instead of assuming a fixed 1/60 second step. BotDriver receives input exactly once per call, so theta in the hoop skill advances only in auto() and never in step(). A compact lines file will enforce a fifty-two character maximum per line, with a script verifying length after generation.\n\nI am writing the lines.js file as an array of short, hand-crafted sentences. Each line will stay under fifty-two characters. I will compose the text directly in the file and add a length checker at the bottom for development verification.\n\nBarks, story, and questions will be included in the lines file. Original line counts will be exported from the banks excluding a SAMPLES set; the test checks that total original lines are at least one hundred twenty. Simulators will be written into one file and the lines into another. The BotDriver now returns empty input for idle skills without needing the dot. Local depth for reps is handled by the scene when calling sim.step.\n\nThe scene will compute press depth as max(0, y) before each step. The simulator reads this value from input.depth. Reps mode will also accept a continuous rate input to adjust the rotation speed directly.\n\nThe keyboard rate input for reps now scales with the target slope. Pressing S increases depth at the speed of one over zero point four times tempo, while releasing the key holds rotation steady. A DeviceTracker class is also being added to detect pad buttons and stick movement.\n\nDeviceTracker now detects mouse movement and wheel input, and it classifies input as pad, mouse, or keyboard. The default device is pad, so prompts start with PlayStation glyphs. A key press switches the device to keyboard, and a gamepad press switches it back. A test will verify that pressing Cross confirms the pad device and its glyphs, then pressing a key switches to keyboard.\n\nDevice detection resolves conflicts by last writer wins. A mouse move switches the device to mouse and moves focus, so a test can do both at once. Clicking activates the focused or hovered item. I am now writing the Ui class with widgets, focus tracking, and row layout.\n\nThe Ui class now preserves focus when rows are reordered or their IDs change. It provides begin, item, and handle methods for layout and input. Stick repeat logic fires every 0.18 seconds while the Y axis is held above 0.5.\n\nConfirm input now uses wasPressed instead of isDown to prevent frame-by-frame repeats. Mouse clicks are detected via buttonEvents with wasPressed('MouseLeft'). Hovering moves focus to the widget under the pointer. Menu rows scale to 16–18 pixels with 22-pixel pitch, fitting eight rows at a 270-pixel HUD height. The title \"Mint Dial\" at scale 3 is 162 pixels wide, exceeding the 144-pixel available width, so it must be shortened or wrapped.\n\nThe title will scale to 2 on narrow screens and 4 on wide ones. Widget positions will be clamped and text wrapped to fit.\n\nThe \"Controller or keyboard required\" prompt will wrap to fit the available width. Layout will derive from hud.width with a 12-pixel safe margin, and draw functions will record widgets inside that inset area. A pause icon at the top right will act as a clickable widget to toggle playback. The attract timer will accumulate update dt, but I need to verify whether timeScale affects that value before running a quick headless test.\n\nThe attract timer will only accumulate update dt during menu modes, so pause does not advance it. Ten seconds is the exact exit threshold. A four-pixel mouse-jitter threshold prevents phantom exits from the first frame's phantom input. Session mode skips attract entirely. Hub integration is now being added to main.js and app.\n\nmain.js now imports the title, App, World, and Engine modules. It detects touch devices and attempts to connect to the AIHub as a ghost player with seats 1 and 4. The App is created with session info and touch-only flags, then the Engine starts the World inside the app container using fill aspect.\n\nThe Engine is configured with a fixed camera, sky background, and quality set to medium or high. If the AIHub connects successfully, it signals readiness; otherwise it reports failure. Tests that run without a document should import World and App directly rather than main.js.\n\nmain.js now guards engine startup with a document check so tests can import it safely. In app.mount, window keydown and pointerdown listeners are added to handle card mode input: Enter triggers AIHub.ready, M triggers AIHub.more, and any input exits attract mode.\n\nWheel events also trigger pointer input. The Create and Options chord pauses only when Options is pressed while Create is not held. Gamepad button mapping is set at boot. Music volume is configured and the title song is registered and played during mount. Songs are validated with parseSong at import time to prevent boot failures.\n\nSong validation will be deferred to tests. A compact builder function normalizes whitespace and enforces equal step counts between lead and bass tracks.\n\nThe song builder now enforces a total volume of at least 0.03, with the lead and bass tracks summing to 1.12 before final scaling. The step song will run exactly 104 steps, and the long stand track will use the same volume fields while building from near silence into a swell. A hat noise track was considered but dropped to keep the arrangement simple. Work is shifting to writing the large files, starting with sims.js, and a week data array is being drafted.\n\nThe week data array is being populated with four days of goals and opening moves. Day one uses a still test with a tree goal and a heron opening. Day two uses a lean test with a stars goal and a header opening. Day three uses a chase test with a new goal and a step opening. Day four uses a still test with a count goal and tray, press, and fold openings.\n\nThe week data now covers seven days. Day five is a lean test with a beat goal for Dennis and openings to jog, pier, and plank. Day six is a chase test with a three-star goal and the hard opening. Day seven is a still test with an id goal for longstand. The goal checker treats \"and\" as requiring the tree activity plus at least one other, and \"stars\" as any activity with at least n stars.\n\nGoal types are now fully defined. \"new\" means an activity never done today. \"count\" requires at least n activities today. \"beat\" means today's best score or any score above the rival. \"id\" means playing longstand.\n\nUnlock rules are set. Activities open on their unlock day or after week two, or if unlockAll is true. The hard flag opens on day six. Locker opens on day two as a place, not an activity. Drop-in ignores unlocks.\n\nStations are being defined. The Pavilion of Holding Still contains tree, heron, warrior, chair, fold, and plank. The Rep Shed contains squat, lunge, and press.\n\nThe remaining stations are Beat Beach, Lagoon Games, Jogging Path, and The Plaza. Long Stand is only available on day 7. The island menu would contain ten rows, exceeding the eight-row limit.\n\nThe island menu is split into two pages of six items each to stay within the eight-row limit. Page one holds Pavilion, Rep Shed, Beat Beach, Lagoon, Jogging Path, and Next. Page two holds Sweat Jar, Locker, Hall of Numbers, Hammock, and The Long Stand on day seven. The main menu starts with The Week.\n\nThe main menu now lists five rows: The Week, Drop-In Class, Family Hour, Settings, and About & Credits. The pause menu has seven rows by default, but in session mode it shrinks to Resume and Settings only. Dev tools will appear as a sixth tab outside the slider table. A simple paged how-to-play text will be added. Name entry will default focus to Done so Enter starts immediately, with a letter grid available for manual input.\n\nA tap helper is being added for scripted tests. It presses and releases a key in two engine steps so wasPressed registers the press. The guest slots menu will show three empty guests with \"New week\" and a continue option labeled \"Day N\". Done will be the default focus so Enter confirms immediately.\n\nA waitMode helper will poll the app mode for up to 600 steps. Activities will step in batches of 120 so long sessions can break early. The results menu will default focus to Next or Back, and Enter will confirm selection.\n\nThe results menu will default focus to \"Back\" so Enter returns directly to the island. Retry is second and Next is third, matching the plan order.\n\nA `choose` helper will move focus to a target row by pressing Down until the row ID matches, then confirm with Enter. This avoids tedious manual navigation in scripted tests. ArrowDown focus wrapping is confirmed to work.\n\nRow IDs are being added to the island menu so scripted tests can target specific stations like pavilion and hammock. Confirming in the ferry mode will skip it, since the test taps Enter directly. The weigh-in will run automatically with the expert driver skill; the test sets that skill at startup, and the reveal waits for a manual confirm.\n\nThe weigh-in test will wait up to 1200 frames for the app to reach the question, reveal, or island mode. It will then choose an answer if the mode is question, or tap Enter to confirm the reveal. During the 14-second weigh-in measurement, queued presses are ignored so they do not accidentally skip the activity.\n\nA double-confirm bug is being fixed. When a mode change happens on the same frame as an Enter press, the key can confirm both the old menu and the new one. The fix reads wasPressed once per frame inside ui.handle() so only one action fires per step.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8cbcc575-7c84-4919-b6e8-050f4cb9d214-61","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/API.md","offset":2884,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8cbcc575-7c84-4919-b6e8-050f4cb9d214-62","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"interface ModelBuild|object:|problems:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/assets.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":883,"output_tokens":13376,"cache_read_input_tokens":161664,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"3fc13e15-908b-4323-a697-85488eb5caee"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8cbcc575-7c84-4919-b6e8-050f4cb9d214-61","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"2884→### buildModel()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction buildModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: ModelOptions = {}): ModelBuild\\n```\\n\\n2890→### class Catalog\\n\\n- `new Catalog(defs: readonly T[] = [], kind = 'def', tool = '')`\\n- `add(...defs: readonly T[]): this`: Add defs; an id that is already taken throws.\\n- `has(id: string): boolean`\\n- `get(id: string): T`\\n- `all(): T[]`\\n- `find(q: { tags?: readonly string[]; text?: string } = {}): T[]`: Defs with every tag in `tags` whose id, about or tags contain every word of `text`.\\n- `search(text: string, o: SearchOptions = {}): T[]`: Defs ranked by how well they match `text`, best first (`searchDefs`).\\n\\n2900→### interface CatalogEntry\\n\\nDefs by id: the engine's built-in libraries (`MODEL_LIBRARY`, `TEXTURE_LIBRARY`) and a game's\\nown. Ids are how parts, layers and saves refer to defs, so an id names one def per catalog.\\n\\n- `readonly id: string`\\n- `readonly about: string`\\n- `readonly tags: readonly string[]`\\n\\n### checkModel()\\n2910→\\n```ts\\nfunction checkModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: CheckOptions = {}): ModelCheck\\n```\\n\\nEvery check over the model's range: the defaults, then each param at its min and at its max\\n(one at a time), each at seeds 1, 2 and 3 when the parts read the rng. Messages say which\\nbuild they came from unless every build agrees.\\n\\n### decodePng()\\n2920→\\n```ts\\nfunction decodePng(bytes: Uint8Array, inflate: Inflate): RgbaImage\\n```\",\"content_concise\":\"2884→### buildModel()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction buildModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: ModelOptions = {}): ModelBuild\\n```\\n\\n2890→### class Catalog\\n\\n- `new Catalog(defs: readonly T[] = [], kind = 'def', tool = '')`\\n- `add(...defs: readonly T[]): this`: Add defs; an id that is already taken throws.\\n- `has(id: string): boolean`\\n- `get(id: string): T`\\n- `all(): T[]`\\n- `find(q: { tags?: readonly string[]; text?: string } = {}): T[]`: Defs with every tag in `tags` whose id, about or tags contain every word of `text`.\\n- `search(text: string, o: SearchOptions = {}): T[]`: Defs ranked by how well they match `text`, best first (`searchDefs`).\\n\\n2900→### interface CatalogEntry\\n\\nDefs by id: the engine's built-in libraries (`MODEL_LIBRARY`, `TEXTURE_LIBRARY`) and a game's\\nown. Ids are how parts, layers and saves refer to defs, so an id names one def per catalog.\\n\\n- `readonly id: string`\\n- `readonly about: string`\\n- `readonly tags: readonly string[]`\\n\\n### checkModel()\\n2910→\\n```ts\\nfunction checkModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: CheckOptions = {}): ModelCheck\\n```\\n\\nEvery check over the model's range: the defaults, then each param at its min and at its max\\n(one at a time), each at seeds 1, 2 and 3 when the parts read the rng. Messages say which\\nbuild they came from unless every build agrees.\\n\\n### decodePng()\\n2920→\\n```ts\\nfunction decodePng(bytes: Uint8Array, inflate: Inflate): RgbaImage\\n```\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/API.md\",\"offset\":2884,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"### buildModel()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction buildModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: ModelOptions = {}): ModelBuild\\n```\\n\\n### class Catalog\\n\\n- `new Catalog(defs: readonly T[] = [], kind = 'def', tool = '')`\\n- `add(...defs: readonly T[]): this`: Add defs; an id that is already taken throws.\\n- `has(id: string): boolean`\\n- `get(id: string): T`\\n- `all(): T[]`\\n- `find(q: { tags?: readonly string[]; text?: string } = {}): T[]`: Defs with every tag in `tags` whose id, about or tags contain every word of `text`.\\n- `search(text: string, o: SearchOptions = {}): T[]`: Defs ranked by how well they match `text`, best first (`searchDefs`).\\n\\n### interface CatalogEntry\\n\\nDefs by id: the engine's built-in libraries (`MODEL_LIBRARY`, `TEXTURE_LIBRARY`) and a game's\\nown. Ids are how parts, layers and saves refer to defs, so an id names one def per catalog.\\n\\n- `readonly id: string`\\n- `readonly about: string`\\n- `readonly tags: readonly string[]`\\n\\n### checkModel()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction checkModel(idOrDef: string | ModelDef, opts: CheckOptions = {}): ModelCheck\\n```\\n\\nEvery check over the model's range: the defaults, then each param at its min and at its max\\n(one at a time), each at seeds 1, 2 and 3 when the parts read the rng. Messages say which\\nbuild they came from unless every build agrees.\\n\\n### decodePng()\\n\\n```ts\\nfunction decodePng(bytes: Uint8Array, inflate: Inflate): RgbaImage\\n```\\n\",\"total_lines\":5143}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-8cbcc575-7c84-4919-b6e8-050f4cb9d214-62","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,49,54,48,55,95,95,120,54,57,56,49,101,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"d58d645e-5e28-4814-b561-1dcf12075206"}
