{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_80","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Reviewing the current status: expert 3 stars, idle 0, sloppy 0, average 1, good 3.\n\nHard and Easy are confirmed at 3. Idle best is 10.27, well below the 18 threshold. The tight controller explains identical jumps for good and expert modes. Next steps are writing the full game, adding tray boards, patching TraySim to select boards by ID, and generating all source files in large batches.\n\nPlanning the remaining source files: UI font utilities, core device and actions, audio modules for voice, songs, and SFX, data for lines and about text, generated credits, and art assets including models, clips, avatar dressing, and glyphs.\n\nThe remaining source files are now mapped out: textures, the main app, world scene, entry point, and build/test/shot tools. Tray boards will be patched immediately. A clinical-text generator will produce the lines data. The glyph charset is being defined for the HUD text system.\n\nThe allowed glyph set is finalized: A-Z, 0-9, and . , ! ? ' : ; - ( ) / + % *. Smart quotes, ampersands, and em dashes are excluded. The generator will enforce a 52-character limit per sentence. Activity names are also being checked for forbidden characters.\n\nEXTRA lines will be generated by a function returning objects with id, speaker, text, and tags. The total line count is 136 star lines plus 20 barks, leaving room for the 120 sample slots. A Nintendo scan will check all player-facing text for forbidden terms. Week beats and UI labels are being reviewed for bad characters. The app.js structure is now being planned.\n\nThe main App class is being drafted as a single file of roughly 1500 lines. It will manage save data, settings, tunables, device state, and UI state including rows, focus, widgets, guest, and slot.\n\nThe App class draft now includes game state fields: driver skill, god mode, pause, time scale, simulation and weighing objects, idle counter, attract mode, session flags, page index, hard mode, overlay, return mode, RNG seed, line queue, medley, card state, hub score, and result sent flag.\n\nThe App class draft now includes game state flags for started and ended, plus arrays for ghosts and pending actions. UI page indices are defined for dialogue, about, and island views. Character state fields include name, look, station, and dropin status. Felt emotion is initialized to 40.\n\nThe App class draft is being completed with prompt text, glyph set, and device state fields. The bind method is also drafted: it configures the canvas context, sets gamepad deadzone, applies audio volumes from settings, boots songs, and routes to either the title screen or card entry based on session mode.\n\nThe `enter` method will set rows, focus, idle state, and music. The `frame` loop resets the action flag, updates the device, and branches between drawing, card input, attract input, pause handling, and gameplay modes.\n\nThe `frame` loop now calls `draw` and `syncMusic`. Pause input triggers on P or Options during activity, island, or weigh modes. The `fixed` loop skips paused or frozen states. It steps the weighing simulation and finishes it when done. It also processes activity input.\n\nThe `fixed` loop now steps the activity simulation and finishes it when done. It also steps the medley, attract, and island hero logic.\n\nThe `inputFor` helper routes bot drivers to `sim.auto(dt)` and human drivers to `LocalDriver`. Menus bypass this helper entirely.\n\nWeigh mode will use the same auto routing as activities.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-4e2653f0-801f-4f8e-8ec4-f188d70ed938-78","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/core/sims.js","offset":202,"limit":70}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":687,"output_tokens":2257,"cache_read_input_tokens":158592,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"fb6ed015-1412-49bc-8211-d3c621662c86"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4e2653f0-801f-4f8e-8ec4-f188d70ed938-78","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"202→export class RepSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\\n    this.announced = this.def.reps;\\n    this.total = this.def.reps + 2;\\n    this.r = 0;\\n    this.err = 0;\\n    this.n = 0;\\n    this.rep = 0;\\n210→    this.gag = '';\\n  }\\n  tempo() {\\n    return (this.def.tempo / (this.T.repTempoScale || 1)) * (this.hard ? 0.85 : 1);\\n  }\\n  playFinished() {\\n    return this.playT >= this.total * this.tempo();\\n  }\\n  auto(dt) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n220→    const uRep = this.playT > 0 ? (this.playT % tempo) / tempo : 0;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const slope = repSlope(uRep) / tempo;\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = 0;\\n    if (this.skill === 'expert' || this.skill === 'good') depth = clamp(target + slope * lag, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'average') depth = clamp(target * 0.75, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'sloppy') depth = clamp(target * 0.25 + 0.1, 0, 1);\\n    const side = this.rep % 2 === 0 ? 1 : -1;\\n    return { ...ZERO, depth, x: this.def.axis === 'side' ? side * depth : 0, y: this.def.axis === 'up' ? depth : -depth };\\n230→  }\\n  onStep(dt, u, scoring) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n    if (scoring) this.playT += dt;\\n    const clock = scoring ? this.playT : this.phaseT;\\n    const rep = Math.min(this.total - 1, Math.floor(clock / tempo));\\n    if (rep !== this.rep) {\\n      this.rep = rep;\\n      const n = rep + 1;\\n      if (n === this.announced) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n240→      else if (n === this.announced + 1) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n      else if (n === this.announced + 2) {\\n        this.gag = 'I lied. This is the last one.';\\n        this.lied = true;\\n      }\\n    }\\n    const uRep = (clock % tempo) / tempo;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = u.depth || 0;\\n250→    if (u.useRate) {\\n      this.r = clamp(this.r + (u.rate || 0) * dt, 0, 1);\\n    } else {\\n      const k = Math.min(1, dt / lag);\\n      this.r += (depth - this.r) * k;\\n    }\\n    const amp = (this.T.repFatigue || 0) * (rep / this.total) ** 2;\\n    this.r = clamp(this.r + amp * Math.sin(40 * clock) * dt * 8, 0, 1);\\n    if (scoring && uRep < 0.9) {\\n      this.err += Math.abs(this.r - target);\\n260→      this.n += 1;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  scoreStars() {\\n    const mean = this.n ? this.err / this.n : 1;\\n    this.score = clamp(1 - 1.6 * mean, 0, 1) * 100;\\n    super.scoreStars();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n270→export class HoopSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\",\"content_concise\":\"202→export class RepSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\\n    this.announced = this.def.reps;\\n    this.total = this.def.reps + 2;\\n    this.r = 0;\\n    this.err = 0;\\n    this.n = 0;\\n    this.rep = 0;\\n210→    this.gag = '';\\n  }\\n  tempo() {\\n    return (this.def.tempo / (this.T.repTempoScale || 1)) * (this.hard ? 0.85 : 1);\\n  }\\n  playFinished() {\\n    return this.playT >= this.total * this.tempo();\\n  }\\n  auto(dt) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n220→    const uRep = this.playT > 0 ? (this.playT % tempo) / tempo : 0;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const slope = repSlope(uRep) / tempo;\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = 0;\\n    if (this.skill === 'expert' || this.skill === 'good') depth = clamp(target + slope * lag, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'average') depth = clamp(target * 0.75, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'sloppy') depth = clamp(target * 0.25 + 0.1, 0, 1);\\n    const side = this.rep % 2 === 0 ? 1 : -1;\\n    return { ...ZERO, depth, x: this.def.axis === 'side' ? side * depth : 0, y: this.def.axis === 'up' ? depth : -depth };\\n230→  }\\n  onStep(dt, u, scoring) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n    if (scoring) this.playT += dt;\\n    const clock = scoring ? this.playT : this.phaseT;\\n    const rep = Math.min(this.total - 1, Math.floor(clock / tempo));\\n    if (rep !== this.rep) {\\n      this.rep = rep;\\n      const n = rep + 1;\\n      if (n === this.announced) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n240→      else if (n === this.announced + 1) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n      else if (n === this.announced + 2) {\\n        this.gag = 'I lied. This is the last one.';\\n        this.lied = true;\\n      }\\n    }\\n    const uRep = (clock % tempo) / tempo;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = u.depth || 0;\\n250→    if (u.useRate) {\\n      this.r = clamp(this.r + (u.rate || 0) * dt, 0, 1);\\n    } else {\\n      const k = Math.min(1, dt / lag);\\n      this.r += (depth - this.r) * k;\\n    }\\n    const amp = (this.T.repFatigue || 0) * (rep / this.total) ** 2;\\n    this.r = clamp(this.r + amp * Math.sin(40 * clock) * dt * 8, 0, 1);\\n    if (scoring && uRep < 0.9) {\\n      this.err += Math.abs(this.r - target);\\n260→      this.n += 1;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  scoreStars() {\\n    const mean = this.n ? this.err / this.n : 1;\\n    this.score = clamp(1 - 1.6 * mean, 0, 1) * 100;\\n    super.scoreStars();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n270→export class HoopSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/core/sims.js\",\"offset\":202,\"limit\":70,\"raw_output\":\"export class RepSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\\n    this.announced = this.def.reps;\\n    this.total = this.def.reps + 2;\\n    this.r = 0;\\n    this.err = 0;\\n    this.n = 0;\\n    this.rep = 0;\\n    this.gag = '';\\n  }\\n  tempo() {\\n    return (this.def.tempo / (this.T.repTempoScale || 1)) * (this.hard ? 0.85 : 1);\\n  }\\n  playFinished() {\\n    return this.playT >= this.total * this.tempo();\\n  }\\n  auto(dt) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n    const uRep = this.playT > 0 ? (this.playT % tempo) / tempo : 0;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const slope = repSlope(uRep) / tempo;\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = 0;\\n    if (this.skill === 'expert' || this.skill === 'good') depth = clamp(target + slope * lag, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'average') depth = clamp(target * 0.75, 0, 1);\\n    else if (this.skill === 'sloppy') depth = clamp(target * 0.25 + 0.1, 0, 1);\\n    const side = this.rep % 2 === 0 ? 1 : -1;\\n    return { ...ZERO, depth, x: this.def.axis === 'side' ? side * depth : 0, y: this.def.axis === 'up' ? depth : -depth };\\n  }\\n  onStep(dt, u, scoring) {\\n    const tempo = this.tempo();\\n    if (scoring) this.playT += dt;\\n    const clock = scoring ? this.playT : this.phaseT;\\n    const rep = Math.min(this.total - 1, Math.floor(clock / tempo));\\n    if (rep !== this.rep) {\\n      this.rep = rep;\\n      const n = rep + 1;\\n      if (n === this.announced) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n      else if (n === this.announced + 1) this.gag = 'Last one.';\\n      else if (n === this.announced + 2) {\\n        this.gag = 'I lied. This is the last one.';\\n        this.lied = true;\\n      }\\n    }\\n    const uRep = (clock % tempo) / tempo;\\n    const target = repTarget(uRep);\\n    const lag = (this.T.repLag || 0.1) * (this.def.lag || 1);\\n    let depth = u.depth || 0;\\n    if (u.useRate) {\\n      this.r = clamp(this.r + (u.rate || 0) * dt, 0, 1);\\n    } else {\\n      const k = Math.min(1, dt / lag);\\n      this.r += (depth - this.r) * k;\\n    }\\n    const amp = (this.T.repFatigue || 0) * (rep / this.total) ** 2;\\n    this.r = clamp(this.r + amp * Math.sin(40 * clock) * dt * 8, 0, 1);\\n    if (scoring && uRep < 0.9) {\\n      this.err += Math.abs(this.r - target);\\n      this.n += 1;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  scoreStars() {\\n    const mean = this.n ? this.err / this.n : 1;\\n    this.score = clamp(1 - 1.6 * mean, 0, 1) * 100;\\n    super.scoreStars();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nexport class HoopSim extends Sim {\\n  _built() {\\n\",\"total_lines\":1064}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"bee65885-688a-454a-84ff-17719d844d37"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_81","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Bots set depth directly. Humans use stick depth.\n\nLocalDriver currently maps downward lean to positive depth. Overhead press requires upward lean instead. Depth should be set based on the input axis in inputFor.\n\nThe depth mapping now handles up, side, and default axes. Keyboard reps will use rate-based input. Now writing the files and implementing finishActivity.\n\nfinishActivity marks the prescription and calculates drips. It updates guest stats including total stars, today's plays and IDs, best star, and per-simulation records.\n\nfinishActivity now updates the guest record's star total and beats the Dennis rival. It refreshes items, persists data, stores the result, and switches to results mode. markPrescription returns true only for the activity that completes the daily goal. prescriptionMet checks the day's goal against today's stats.\n\nprescriptionMet now covers all goal kinds: pair, stars, fresh, count, beat, and id. A bonus applies only when this activity is the one that finally completes the goal. On Day 1, the tree activity alone does not finish the pair goal; the squat activity triggers the bonus.\n\nFresh goal logic checks that today's play count is exactly one after incrementing. Hammock mode shows a stay message if the guest has zero plays, then enters confession on day seven only if they have completed longstand and not yet confessed; otherwise it enters report. Confession confirmation subtracts seven days from every chart entry and marks the guest as confessed.\n\nConfession confirmation advances the guest to day eight and week two, then enters the weigh screen. The plan calls for an evening report before confession.\n\nThe report screen now routes directly to confession on day seven if the guest has completed longstand and not yet confessed. Otherwise it advances to the next day. The nextDay method increments the day, upgrades to week two after day seven, resets today's stats, persists the data, and enters the weigh screen. The weigh finish handler stores the T-score in the guest's samples and begins calculating true age from those measurements.\n\nThe weigh finish calculates true age from the T-score and adds seven days if the guest has not yet confessed. It stores the day, T-score, true age, and shown age in the chart before entering the question screen.\n\nThe question screen stores the remark and proceeds to reveal. Reveal displays the shown age; confirming advances to the island.\n\nThe full flow is name, ferry on day one only, weigh, question, reveal, then island. Day two onward skips the ferry entirely.\n\nA `place` field will track the guest's current phase—such as weigh, island, report, or ferry—to resume from the correct screen on continue. New slots initialize with day one and empty stats.\n\nDrop-in mode will store its own records in save.data instead of using a guest object, skipping drips and story entirely. Family hour also runs without a guest. A developer shortcut to jump directly to any day is being added.\n\nA developer helper sets the current simulation to a given star count and immediately finishes the activity. Another shortcut adds fifty drips to the guest and refreshes their items. A third shortcut unlocks all content by advancing the guest to day seven or setting an unlock flag.\n\nAutopilot, god mode, time scale, overlay toggle, HUD hide, free camera, dialogue tester, sound test, animation viewer, and save reset are all being wired up as developer shortcuts. The unlockAll flag will bypass locked content checks.\n\nThe developer settings tab mutates the shared `tunables` object directly, so live tuning works during an activity. Resetting a single tab key restores its default value, while resetting all replaces each property in place without replacing the object itself. The music slider adjusts `settings.music` by 0.05 and clamps it between zero and one. The gentle toggle is stored in `settings.gentle` and passed to the simulation as a flag.\n\nThe attract mode will loop through a sequence of hub screens and activity clips. It creates simulations with expert difficulty, god mode, doubled ring scale, and practice disabled. Each clip runs for its assigned seconds before advancing. Any input during attract returns the player to the title screen. The idle timer increments on menu screens when no input is detected.\n\nIdle time on menu screens triggers attract mode after ten seconds. Input counts as a device change, menu action, button press, mouse delta, or pad axis. Touch-only mode ignores confirm actions that would leave the title and shows a message instead; it disables once the device switches to keyboard or pad. The card screen draws the title, players, ready state, goal, controls, and remaining time.\n\nA keydown listener is being wired into main.js. The medley mode will run a local multi-lane race with bots, pads, keys, and ghosts. Each lane tracks its driver type, skill level, simulation, scores, and ghost state. The step function will use the session clock.\n\nThe medley step function advances the local timer when no session is active. It checks the current window phase and finishes the race if it is done. Ghost lanes are updated without stepping their simulations. For active lanes, a new simulation is created whenever the window index changes during the play phase, using the lane's skill level. Simulations are only stepped while the window phase is play.\n\nActive medley lanes step their simulations and record normalized scores. Gap-phase lanes save the final score and discard the simulation. In session mode, our lane is simulated while others are ghosted from messages; we send our state. A local four-bot test can be started from a \"Fill with classmates\" join screen. For browser races, keyboard and two pads will join; pad input will be polled via getGamepads.\n\nPad input polls four gamepads and detects button presses to claim seats on the join screen. The Enter key claims a seat for keyboard players. The start row is enabled once at least one seat is filled. Browser tests will simulate pad presses through stub pads, and both the engine's PadCross event and the custom pad press handler may fire.\n\nPad seats are claimed via pollPads, and Enter claims a keyboard seat only if none is taken. During medley, lane zero uses pad axes, lane one uses another pad, and lane two uses keyboard; their dots should diverge. The test will step a few frames and compare each lane's dot position. Medley starts in the play phase, but simulations still step during intro while the dot remains frozen.\n\nA `skipIntro` option will be added to the Sim constructor so medley simulations can bypass the two-second intro and start directly in play. Medley will pass this flag when creating its lanes.\n\nA ghost lane test will verify that a non-stepping Dot marker stays at time zero. Session ghosts receive state updates via message pushes and present them each frame without creating simulations. The bind function also handles the AIHub start event to enter medley mode.\n\nThe session medley starts immediately on the AIHub start event. A 100-millisecond hub beat loop sends lane state and score to the hub. The time-up event triggers result submission. The end event stops the timer and sets an ended flag.\n\nThe end handler also stops the music. The leave event marks a ghost as departed. Incoming messages are routed to the matching ghost driver. The human lane in a session may score zero if the harness does not send input.\n\nGhost lanes must still send state and report progress even when the score is zero. The harness auto-mode may not play the activity well, but submitting a zero result is acceptable.\n\nMedley picks will use AIHub.rng('picks') instead of Math.random. The card screen will use AIHub.rng('card') for its bobbing needle animation.\n\nThe ready() function will be called from main.js after Engine.start, and also from World.ready() as a fallback.\n\nWorld.setup must finish before Engine.start resolves so the card can appear. Setup is fast because the ounce buildModel is synchronous and cheap. If WebGPU fails, the engine start throws and the code catches it to report the error to AIHub.\n\n`__GAME__` is set in `World.ready` so headless tests can reach the App via both global and local references. `state()` returns a JSON-safe object, `fingerprint()` encodes mode, score, dot, and day into a string, and `status()` produces a single-line summary. The `go(id, args)` method routes to `startActivity` for activity IDs and to `enter('title')` for title.\n\nThe `go` method routes day changes to the island screen, medley starts directly, and attract enters the attract mode. The `setDriver` method updates the lane skill, sets the simulation skill, and rebuilds the weigh bot with the new skill level.\n\nMenu rows rebuild only when the mode or page changes, and on entry, to keep focus stable during arrow navigation. Tests can target a row by its id. The title row is 'start' at focus 0 and Enter confirms it. Slider labels update dynamically as their values change.\n\nMenu rows will update their labels in place when the user presses left or right, without resetting focus. The draw method records widget rectangles by id and position so tests can verify layout. Layout uses a 12-pixel safe margin, but shrinks to 4 pixels when the screen width drops below 200 pixels. Row width equals the HUD width minus twice the margin.\n\nRow height is 12 pixels for the title block and 18 pixels for other rows. The layout must fit within 240 to 270 pixels of vertical space. Art assets scale down to 480 by 270, 427 by 240, or 320 by 180 depending on resolution. say() will wrap text line by line. The title string must appear in the HUD. Touch messages are wrapped. Stars are drawn as filled sprites instead of characters; the state stores a star count and the HUD renders that many filled circles. The prompt sits at the bottom with a glyph and verb.\n\nA simple cross sprite will represent the PlayStation pad. The ounce model and several other simple models need to be written, then tested with checkModel. Thin parts may be flagged as zero-problem issues, so they will be thickened to 0.06 on the smallest two dimensions if necessary while keeping the design intact. Clip files will also be written and validated, though analyzeClip requires a hero.\n\nTray boards will be added. Files will be written in parallel batches using the Write tool. A node one-liner will generate creditsText.js. The about.js module will export source metadata, testing and engine feedback arrays, AI hub configuration, and a medical disclaimer.\n\nabout.js will avoid importing pixel-engine so build.mjs stays lightweight. The tech string will list Pixel Engine 0.46.0, three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, and esbuild 0.28.2. A runtime ENGINE_VERSION check will supply the engine version for the screen. A pages() helper will return about content as string arrays to verify the medical disclaimer and source fields appear.\n\nThe about screen will expose joined text in `state().aboutText` so tests can verify the medical disclaimer and feedback headings. The exit interview will be generated by build.mjs and also exported as a function for test re-parsing. The hand-in files must exist before the user runs build, so I will run the build step now. A PROGRESS.md will be written at the end to summarize completed work.\n\nTray boards will cycle through two winnable designs using the same structure but different x positions. The station menu will gain a board selector row so the player can change the tray layout with left and right.\n\nThe tray board selector will sit on the tray station as a non-launching row that cycles with left and right. Existing act-tray tests will remain valid. The hit radius is already 0.2, so it does not need shrinking when a seed scores a star. Hard activity thresholds require two stars; expert-level tree scoring may drop to one star because the ring shrink factor of 0.85 reduces the effective hit radius to 0.34.\n\nSloppy thresholds will be verified against expert for each activity and corrected if inverted. The jog idle star bug is confirmed fixed. Practice mode will remain enabled for the first playthrough to match the real game, though it costs eight seconds of non-scoring time. The test timeout must be set high enough to accommodate practice plus hold.\n\nActivity durations were estimated to set test timeouts. Tree takes about 45 seconds, long stand about 104, step about 90, and hoop about 75. The test will wait up to 120 seconds per activity. Day one will use squat instead of hoop to save time, since the prescription requires tree plus one other activity.\n\nDay 4 will use three tray plays to satisfy the three-activity requirement. Tray is fast enough to complete in about seventeen seconds per session, so three fits comfortably within the day. Day 5 will beat Dennis with one expert tray. Day 6 will also use one tray to earn three stars.\n\nDay 2 will use one squat or tree to earn two stars. Day 3 will use a fresh activity such as heron. The unlock test will advance the week and assert the header appears on day 2. Items will be granted at milestones based on drip count, and the guest items array will be checked in tests.\n\nSoundDef requires wave, frequency, decay, and volume. Two tracks will sum to 0.084, satisfying the minimum volume threshold. The step song will run at 104 BPM. The long stand song will use a sparse pattern followed by a swell; because a long pattern is hard to write, the swell will be a second loop in the order that plays later.\n\nThe long stand song will use 22 sparse patterns followed by a swell pattern. At 70 BPM with 4 steps per beat, each pattern lasts about 3.4 seconds, so the chord lands near the 75-second mark.\n\nA `hold` helper will generate long step strings by repeating a note for many steps. The sparse pattern will use mostly rests with a soft note every eight steps, totaling 352 steps. `parseSong` requires all tracks to have the same length. The full SFX list is now defined.\n\nThe `whirr` SFX will loop via `audio.loop` and expose a play function. The constructor sets the intro queue before `skipIntro` can override it, so the override must be inserted after the queue is built.\n\nThe skipIntro override resets the queue index to zero. A gamepad button map constant will be added to actions.js. Files will be written in this order: font, voice, songs, sfx, device, actions, lines, models, clips, avatar, glyphs, textures, about, then app, world, main, and tools. Press clip poses will mirror the plan exactly.\n\nPress clip poses are being defined with pelvis and arm offsets. Lunge left will use a mirrored variant. StepL and StepR clips are drafted with foot placement, though they may fail model analysis if the feet sink.\n\nOnly clips that pass model analysis will be shipped. The Press clip will mirror the squat structure, and Step can be a simple hold pose. Any extra clips that fail analysis will be dropped. The avatar dress needs THREE imported from pixel-engine.\n\nThe avatar dress will import THREE from pixel-engine.js. World setup will wrap hero creation in try/catch so a failed dress does not blank the game; tests must assert that engine.errors stays empty. The cameraTarget method must return a Vector3.\n\nThe camera target will be a single Vector3 instance. The world name will be set to TITLE. Aspect fill and background will be configured. The test runner will collect failures in an array, print only the failed assertions, and exit with a non-zero code if any tests fail.\n\nA `check` helper will record failed assertions by name and optional detail. The test runner will print only failures and a one-line pass count on failure, or just the count on success. A `--fast` flag will skip browser and hub tests because they are slow.\n\nThe scratch directory will contain test.log, browser.log, hub.log, nosession.log, boot screenshots, and a shots folder. The test runner will tee stdout into test.log while writing its own browser logs internally.\n\nThe test runner will write scratch files itself while the outer shell captures stdout. Playwright is available in the dev folder node_modules. Browser flags enable unsafe WebGPU and SwiftShader Vulkan. HTML pages will be served via file:// URLs. Two browser launches are permitted, but sequential launches with one browser and two contexts are safer for memory.\n\nThe browser harness will run sequentially: one page per browser, closing each before the next. The hub harness also uses a browser and must be handled the same way.\n\nScreenshots will be captured via `page.evaluate` using a recipe function. A blank-check will decode the PNG and count non-background pixels; if capture fails, it writes `capture-error.txt`.\n\nMusic volume will be read with `page.evaluate(() => window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__.audio.volume.music)`, and the title will be read with `page.evaluate(() => window.__GAME__.state())`.\n\nPage errors and severe console messages will be collected and treated as test failures. A gamepad stub script will run before navigation. The browser harness will use the iPhone 13 Playwright device descriptor, falling back to listing available devices if it is missing.\n\nWebKit iPhone and Firefox will be tested because WebGPU is unavailable on those browsers. The audio analyser must connect to the engine's master bus after a click, but the master field is private and cannot be read directly. I will inspect the engine object in the browser test to find the correct audio node key.\n\nThe audio analyser connection will be found by inspecting the engine object in the browser. The code will try `a.master`, then `a.buses?.master`, then `a.buses?.music`. Voice will be tested with OfflineAudioContext.\n\nThe TypeScript `private` keyword is erased at runtime, so `engine.audio.master` and `engine.audio.buses` are real properties. The test will try connecting the analyser to either one and adapt if needed.\n\nA widget test will resize the viewport, step frames, and read widgets and HUD values. The family hour browser test will navigate the UI with keyboard input and stub gamepad pads; it is complex and will be written carefully and iterated.\n\nThe hub harness will use a Node HTTP server serving a temporary directory inside the dev folder.\n\nThe hub harness will use a `.hub-serve` directory inside the dev folder. It will be deleted after the test to avoid leaving junk. The server will set `Content-Type: text/javascript` for `.js` files and `text/html` for `.html`. Four sequential pages will each time out after about 60 seconds. The total hub test should take two to four minutes. The title must read \"Mint Dial\" before the harness trusts the page.\n\nA no-session test will load game.html directly in title mode with AIHub.active set to false. The hub harness will kill only its own server PID. A new shot.mjs tool will capture evidence screenshots at 1920 resolution for title, activity, island, and pause states, and it will run during verification. The goal plan checklist will be updated at the end, along with a deviations log.\n\nThe game will be a single World with modes living in App rather than scene files. Vitamin Tray is kinematic and ships three boards: straight, split, and wide. Pier flight recentres the dot when a run ends so air balance starts from a stand instead of a crouch. Attract and medley simulations skip the intro queue to begin directly on action. The three boards will be added now, followed by file writes while keeping app.js correct. engine.timeScale resets to 1 on level unload, but that is harmless since there is only one level. Pause will set engine.timeScale to 0.\n\nThe pause overlay will save the current timeScale and mode before freezing the game. Resuming restores both. The dev menu can open from pause without destroying the simulation. Returning from dev returns to the previous pause state.\n\nSettings can open from pause without destroying the simulation; returning from settings restores the paused state. The attract demo will be suppressed while paused. Island mode will load the hero model during setup, but only when no session is active to keep ready() fast.\n\nThe island hero model will be loaded during setup only when no session is active. PlatformerCharacter creation on island entry will use that stored model, and mid-game physics characters should work. Stations will trigger via proximity checks each frame; crossing a station unlocks it. The carousel menu will serve as the main UI for station selection.\n\nStation triggers will be created in setup even if the hero model fails to load, so the test can still verify they exist. The PlatformerCharacter tag matches the trigger tag used in the example.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b58a13ed-befe-4a40-9c55-a05a8786534e-79","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"update\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/character/PlatformerCharacter.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3226,"output_tokens":10649,"cache_read_input_tokens":159232,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"f1b81699-0983-4336-a276-67ff450b78b4"}
