,46,114,110,103,32,61,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,40,108,97,98,101,108,41,32,123,10,51,56,56,45,32,32,32,32,118,97,114,32,104,32,61,32,50,49,54,54,49,51,54,50,54,49,59,10,51,56,57,45,32,32,32,32,118,97,114,32,115,32,61,32,83,116,114,105,110,103,40,108,97,98,101,108,32,61,61,61,32,117,110,100,101,102,105,110,101,100,32,63,32,39,39,32,58,32,108,97,98,101,108,41,59,10,51,57,48,45,32,32,32,32,102,111,114,32,40,118,97,114,32,105,32,61,32,48,59,32,105,32,60,32,115,46,108,101,110,103,116,104,59,32,105,43,43,41,32,123,10,51,57,49,45,32,32,32,32,32,32,104,32,94,61,32,115,46,99,104,97,114,67,111,100,101,65,116,40,105,41,59,10,51,57,50,45,32,32,32,32,32,32,104,32,61,32,77,97,116,104,46,105,109,117,108,40,104,44,32,49,54,55,55,55,54,49,57,41,59,10,51,57,51,45,32,32,32,32,125,10,51,57,52,45,32,32,32,32,114,101,116,117,114,110,32,103,101,110,101,114,97,116,111,114,40,40,104,117,98,46,115,101,101,100,32,94,32,104,41,32,62,62,62,32,48,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/vendor/hub/session.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":382,\"content\":\"  hub.time = function () {\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"f784acc9-adfd-4161-baf9-683729d341f5"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_121","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I will now write the files.\n\nI'll write a comprehensive app.js. I'll cycle through the four look presets and set pixel size to chunky. I'll use defensive code with try-catch blocks.\n\nI'll write src/data/about.js with honest feedback. I'll update the issue count after tests. Four prior bugs are fixed: Pier expert scoring, Step and lagoon song parsing, tray board lookup scope, and model part z-fighting.\n\nThe about.js feedback will note that testing caught the four known issues plus any suite failures. The bot star sweep was the most helpful test; it caught the critical pier scoring bug. The glyph scan was least helpful because it passed immediately and changed nothing.\n\nI'll generate the credits text file with a Node.js one-liner. It reads CREDITS.md and writes a JavaScript module with the full text and a brief summary of asset licenses.\n\nThe dev folder's package.json already uses ESM, so the Node one-liner will run from there. I'll keep app.js compact under 1000 lines. I'm now sketching the main modules: World.js for drawing and 3D, main.js for boot, and a test boot helper that instantiates App, World, and a headless Engine.\n\nThe test boot helper will use a fixed camera preset at [9, 7, 11] aimed at [0, 1, 0]. It returns the app, engine, and world. Headless EngineOptions supports viewport and camera settings. The game setup will attach the engine to app.ctx. Engines must be disposed between tests to prevent memory leaks.\n\nAll node tests will run sequentially on a single headless engine. Pure tests that need no engine will run first. Then one engine boots for all gameplay tests. The week test requires a fresh save, so the app will reset or clear the save at its start. Activity tests will set the driver and enter the activity.\n\nAfter each test the mode is results. Next go activity replaces the simulation.\n\nThe attract test needs to run on the title screen with idle. After other tests I will return to title and reset idle.\n\nThe pause test runs during an activity.\n\nI will reset deliberately if one engine's state gets corrupted.\n\nThe replay test records steps then replays them. It loads a new World into a fresh App because the old world is no longer __GAME__.\n\nThe replay test will record the engine, step it to a fixed point, and capture its fingerprint. After replaying the recording into a fresh App and World, the new fingerprint must match the original. The original world’s random state is already embedded in the recording seed.\n\nI will avoid random setup steps so the replay fingerprint stays deterministic. The replay test will not be skipped if it fails; I will fix it. Optional snapshot methods that copy app state JSON are also being considered.\n\nSnapshot and restore are unnecessary because the replay test does not use them. Hero physics bodies will match across replays since the hero stands still and produces no input. Palm positions are fixed. The hero compile may be slow or throw, so it will be wrapped in try-catch. Idle animation frames are deterministic enough to keep the fingerprint stable.\n\nThe dev slider test will mutate tunables like dotInstability and step the simulation to verify that dot noise changes live. The unused name in lines.js will be skipped.\n\nThe dev slider test will verify live behavior by mutating app.tunables directly. It will zero out dotInstability, dotNoise, and dotAuthority, reset the dot velocity, step the simulation, and confirm the dot barely moves compared to defaults.\n\nThe dev slider test will zero out dotInstability, dotNoise, and dotAuthority while setting dotDamping to 8. It will step the simulation with the dot at x=0.2 and velocity zero, then assert the dot barely moves because damping cannot change a stopped velocity. Reset-all will restore default values, and reset-tab will only restore its own tab’s keys. I will also add a nudge helper to apply the step from the table and verify that DOT_DEFAULTS is not double-set.\n\nAbout.js will generate its pages from a single source of truth. The screen will page through the content, with each page showing up to eight lines. About lines will wrap at fifty-two characters.\n\nThe medical sentence will be its own about page. app.js will be written in a single call. Engine.headless awaits async setup. A truthy session skips hero loading. Node tests use null session. Touch-only tests will tap Enter on the title screen, assert the controller prompt, and step to attract.\n\nThe settings button will be positioned at the top-right in touch-only mode and hidden in node tests. main.js will pass `button: touchOnly ? 'top-right' : false` to the engine settings.\n\nHeadless tests construct World directly, so withUrlOptions is only needed when document exists. The browser bundle will export boot for the HTML entry point. esbuild will embed pixel-engine into the HTML, keeping the file around five megabytes. session.js is pasted as a standalone script before the module loads, so the module must reference globalThis.AIHub rather than importing it to avoid duplication. HUB metas will be placed exactly in the HTML.\n\nNode tests will import app.js and world.js directly, avoiding main.js to prevent an unwanted browser boot. The build script can safely import about.js because it contains no pixel-engine dependencies.\n\nI will keep about.js free of direct credits imports so world can handle both. I double-checked the song step token counts and confirmed they are correct. I will re-validate models after writing if warnings remain, but tests will catch any issues. I am now composing the large source files carefully. I will also add a small UI drawing helper to world to keep app.js free of HUD logic.\n\nApp widgets are cleared each draw by World. Drawing logic will live in world.js as functions rather than a layout helper. Menu labels use plain text glyphs only. The star row shows a number plus sprite pips. Confession chart mutations happen exactly once. Week numbers split at day eight.\n\nThe day transition logic is now fully defined. nextDay increments the guest day, assigns week two once the day reaches eight, clears today’s activities, persists the save, and returns to the weigh screen. On day seven, sleeping with longstand triggers the confession screen; otherwise the game proceeds to nextDay. Confession confirmation subtracts seven days and advances to credits, while credits confirmation triggers nextDay again, landing on day eight of week two. The story confession is specifically tied to the longstand activity.\n\nThe ferry screen appears before the weigh-in on the first day. After name confirmation, the game checks whether the guest has already been ferried; if not, it loads the ferry, and confirming there sets the flag and returns to weigh. If the guest is new, the name screen creates a fresh guest and saves the slot before deciding which screen to show.\n\nSlot selection resumes the morning after a save. If the guest already weighed today, the game loads the island screen; otherwise it returns to weigh. A fresh slot always starts at the name screen. Reloading mid-week only requires reading storage, so a second engine is unnecessary.\n\nA reload test will instantiate a fresh App with the same storage and verify that guest.day equals two. Corrupt save data will be handled by setting an invalid JSON string in storage; a new Save instance must read every slot as null. When storage is missing, each Save constructor creates its own private memory store, so writes do not persist across separate instances.\n\nA single Save instance with a null storage argument will be tested to confirm that writes and reads share the same private memory store. The App constructor will also be verified to use any storage object passed in via its options.\n\nThe App constructor initializes its state. It merges saved settings into defaults, sets the mode to card or title based on a session argument, and prepares empty arrays and flags for focus, rows, widgets, and guest data.\n\nThe App constructor now calls `this.refresh()` at the end. When a session is active, it skips loading saved guest data and keeps the default music volume of 0.10. World setup reads that value from `app.settings.music`, overwriting any stale engine-level setting. A `tools/shot.mjs` script will be added for screenshots, and the goal plan will be updated only after all files are written.\n\nThe weigh-in timer is 840 frames. Activity completion switches the mode to results inside fixedUpdate, so tests can assert that state after stepping. Skipping the intro immediately enters play, which lasts 1860 frames before the outro begins. The outro adds 1.2 seconds, and skipIntro sets the phase to play with a queue of play followed by outro.\n\nTests will fast-forward using 120-frame step loops, which is sufficient for the longest activity plus its outro. Total test time is estimated at two minutes. After boot, tests will check engine.errors to catch setup failures. Errors during dress or compile may be logged without throwing if they occur after the ready phase.\n\nSetup will catch hero model loading failures in headless mode so tests can continue without the asset. The title screen prompt will read \"Start\"; all other screens will read \"Confirm\". HUD drawing logic will remain in world.js rather than moving to a separate file.\n\nThe state() snapshot will include prompt, glyphs, device, mode, title, music, widgets, touchMessage, day, week, focus, row id, tunables copy, overlay, god, driver, attract, hideHud, confessed, chart shown ages, drips, last stars, activity, lanes positions, ghostSimulated, and timeScale. Widgets are plain objects with id, x, y, w, h. A fit test will verify every widget stays inside the HUD bounds using a margin inset.\n\nTitle text scales at 2× below 220 pixels and 3× below 400 pixels, then 4×. At 480 pixels the centered title is 132 pixels from the left edge. Row labels use scale 2 when the HUD is at least 220 pixels wide, otherwise scale 1. The longest label, \"Pavilion of Holding Still\", measures 27 characters; at scale 2 it spans 324 pixels, which fits at 480 pixels but may exceed a 168-pixel width by two pixels. Shortening labels or using shorter station IDs is being considered to keep everything inside the margin.\n\nThe longest station label, \"Pavilion of Holding Still\", exceeds the 168-pixel menu width even with margins. Shortened labels will be used in the menu rows, and the full name will appear as the screen title when inside a station. The chosen short labels are Pavilion, Rep Shed, Beach, Lagoon, Jog, and Plaza.\n\nThe longest activity name, \"The Chair Nobody Asked For\", is 29 characters. At scale 1 it spans 174 pixels, which exceeds the 168-pixel portrait phone width even with margins. The touch gate message must wrap to fit. On a 168-pixel screen, activity menus may not appear at all because the touch gate blocks play.\n\nThe widget fit test targets 640×360, where aspect-fill art fills the viewport and names fit easily. At 1920×1080 the art resolution stays 480×270.\n\nActivity names fit at scale 2 within the 480-pixel width. The touch gate message will wrap to fit on narrow screens, and menu labels will be kept short enough for a 168-pixel portrait phone. The widget fit test will record widgets and assert they remain inside whatever HUD size the engine reports. A centered text helper is being drafted that measures text width and centers it within the margin bounds.\n\nThe centered text helper will shrink scale to 1 if a line still overflows the margin, leaving wrapping to the caller. Long lines will be wrapped manually. The `drawMenu` implementation is starting with a `marginOf` helper that returns 4 pixels below 200 pixels wide and 12 otherwise, plus a `paint` function that clears the HUD, resets widgets, and begins drawing the panel.\n\nThe HUD will not use a full-screen solid rectangle so the 3D background remains visible for captures. Instead, a dark translucent bottom panel and a top band behind the title will provide readability without obscuring the scene.\n\nThe top panel will be a 80-pixel navy band. Panels drawn with `hud.rect` will also be recorded as widgets so the fit test covers them. The prompt will sit at the bottom of the HUD. Family hour draws four dials, but tests will verify state rather than pixel output. Lane positions will be stored in `state().lanes` as objects containing kind, x, simulated, and t.\n\nThe full test suite will be written after the game boots and crashes are fixed. A minimal boot test will pass first, then expand. The simulation and bot will each receive their own random function so dot phase consumption does not affect bot noise.\n\nTests will exercise the menu-to-tree path to confirm it yields the same stars as the direct go('activity') route for experts. The week flow uses this menu path, so the star table can validate against go().\n\nThe `go('activity')` path and the menu's station selection both call `startActivity`, so they share the same logic. The next step is writing `app.js`. Tests will assert zero problems while logging warnings, since \"clean\" means no errors.\n\nHammock cloth overlaps posts by 0.03. Pier rail overlaps deck by 0.02. Kevin's feet will shift to y=0.12 so the body ball sits 0.01 above the ground and stops floating. The ball dimensions might be radii or diameters.\n\nThe ball dimensions are being verified against test warnings. A rand helper is needed for activity entry before the engine context is ready.\n\nThe `fork()` method will use only the label for its seed, omitting the forks counter so replay stays deterministic regardless of draw order. When the engine context is unavailable, it falls back to a local `mulberry32` stream.\n\nForking the same label twice in one session would reuse the same random sequence, which is acceptable for determinism and repeatable replays. Each activity start should use a unique label that includes a counter so replay restores the correct state.\n\nThe `fork()` method now appends a counter to the label so each activity start gets a unique random stream. The fingerprint is taken before any fork, so replay restarts the counter at zero and the same input produces identical forks.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Models and clips check out. Song patterns that didn’t line up are fixed. Next I’ll add the game shell, the build, and the tests."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5d4a03c9-70cc-4602-9348-a9bb2f845e6b-73","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/art/models.js","old_string":"    { name: 'footL', box: [0.08, 0.1, 0.12], at: [-0.08, 0.1, 0.05], c: 'foot' },\n    { name: 'footR', box: [0.08, 0.1, 0.12], at: [0.08, 0.1, 0.05], c: 'foot' },","new_string":"    { name: 'footL', box: [0.08, 0.12, 0.12], at: [-0.08, 0.12, 0.04], c: 'foot' },\n    { name: 'footR', box: [0.08, 0.12, 0.12], at: [0.08, 0.12, 0.04], c: 'foot' },"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5d4a03c9-70cc-4602-9348-a9bb2f845e6b-74","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607\" && node --input-type=module -e '\nimport fs from \"fs\";\nconst text = fs.readFileSync(\"CREDITS.md\",\"utf8\");\nconst summary = \"Kenney assets are CC0. three.js is MIT. Rapier is Apache-2.0. The Sweetie 16 palette is credited with the kit. A short page, then the full kit text.\";\nfs.writeFileSync(\"src/data/creditsText.js\", \"export const CREDITS_SUMMARY = \" + JSON.stringify(summary) + \";\\nexport const CREDITS_TEXT = \" + JSON.stringify(text) + \";\\n\");\nconsole.log(\"credits\", text.length);\n'","description":"Generate creditsText.js from CREDITS.md"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2968,"output_tokens":7889,"cache_read_input_tokens":165120,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"27b7c0f4-eee6-4c9b-9c28-3dd8c0ebc8b5"}
