step(2);\\nconst s = app.state();\\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify({ mode: s.mode, title: s.title, music: s.music, bus: engine.audio.volume.music, hud: [engine.hud.width, engine.hud.height], widgets: s.widgets.length, row: s.row }));\\nif (s.title !== \\\"Mint Dial\\\") throw new Error(\\\"title\\\");\\nif (Math.abs(engine.audio.volume.music - 0.1) > 1e-6) throw new Error(\\\"music \\\" + engine.audio.volume.music);\\nconsole.log(\\\"boot ok\\\");\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Smoke-boot headless world and check songs\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3%2Fwork/01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469/terminal/call-1116e26f-d3ed-4eff-8b08-ae44f0ac6281-114.log\",\"total_bytes\":542}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"606974e3-fb3f-4d94-9e7e-0e865c7375d3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_157","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Boot succeeded. Songs parsed correctly. Models produced no warnings.\n\nMusic title works. Writing test.mjs now. Will probe activity timing and check model output shape before running the full suite.\n\nPlanning test structure and helpers. Will run dot bands first, then add browser tests after the node suite passes.\n\nDrafting the browser test suite now. Configuring Playwright to launch Chromium with GPU arguments and load the HTML file via pathToFileURL.\n\nDrafting the browser test helper `openGame`. It launches a 960×540 viewport, captures page errors and console messages, waits for `window.__GAME__` and `window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__`, then steps the engine twice.\n\nRefining browser test error handling. Only uncaught exceptions will fail the suite. Console warnings will be filtered out, but genuine engine errors like \"Failed to start\" must still be caught.\n\nDrafting the `grab` helper. It steps the engine 30 frames, captures the renderer output, draws it to a canvas, and overlays the HUD before returning a PNG data URL.\n\nAdding a pixel variance check inside the browser page to detect visual differences between captures. If capture throws, the error is saved and the test proceeds with state. Two boots must agree on title, music 0.10, and mode title.\n\nState snapshots will be compared instead of exact pixel matches to account for timing differences. A second browser context will test gamepad input on the same Chromium instance. A third context will simulate an iPhone 13 using Playwright's device descriptors.\n\niPhone test context uses deviceScaleFactor 1 to keep captures small. Mobile media queries will be tested without rendering. The browser suite runs in sequence: Chromium first, then Webkit, then Firefox. Each browser closes before the next launches.\n\nChromium hub tests will open four URLs sequentially on one page. No session page is needed.\n\nShots at 1920×1080 will run one by one inside the same Chromium session to save memory.\n\nAudio tests require clicking the app first, then unlocking the engine audio context.\n\nVoice offline mode does not need the game audio context.\n\nDrafting the gamepad stub for the browser test. It creates two fake pads with axes and buttons, and a `__pressPads` helper that triggers a rising edge by setting button 0 to pressed.\n\nDrafting a `tap` helper inside `page.evaluate` to simulate key presses by setting a key true, stepping the engine, releasing it, and stepping again. Family hour navigation will use this helper to poll join. Widget size tests will step the engine after resizing the viewport, then read widget and HUD dimensions plus document scroll width.\n\nWidget bounds will be checked against HUD dimensions with a small margin. Title widgets must exist. The phone settings button will be located by its text, aria-label, or title attribute.\n\nAttract mode on phone will step 700 frames. WebGPU software rendering may be slow but acceptable.\n\nTouch gameplay tests will verify engine.touch is null and no touch buttons exist. With state.touchOnly enabled, confirming the title should not leave the title screen while attract continues running.\n\nMusic will be checked at 0.10 in a fresh context to ignore any stale localStorage values.\n\nThe test file is being written now. Node tests will remain robust. Each activity will log to the beat file for monitoring.\n\nHash and data tests do not need the engine. Activity tests will boot once with noHero. The week test will reset state by calling loadGame with a fresh World and App using memory storage. After loadGame, the old app variable becomes stale, so all engine access must use globalThis.__GAME__.\n\nA `game()` helper will read the active app from `globalThis.__GAME__`. The week test will use a replay callback that calls `loadGame` with a fresh in-memory app to reset state deterministically. Need to verify that `loadGame` is async and returns a promise.\n\nActivity fingerprints will be compared after the dot has moved into the activity. The first five frames are skipped because setDriver and go are not input events. Scripted menu entries were considered but rejected as too flaky.\n\nThe activity test will record until the mode becomes an activity and play time exceeds one second. The replay will use that exact frame count. Recording will rely solely on setKey to keep the path human-driven; setDriver is excluded so the dot moves from noise alone. Fingerprinting includes the dot position.\n\nReplay determinism is confirmed. The forked random context restores its seed, so the same frame sequence repeats. Name buffer and focus keys are identical across runs. Recording will use engine.recording() directly without extra storage. A while loop will step the engine until the mode becomes an activity and play time exceeds one second.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-5c20ed04-1d36-4a41-844c-c3ba6cdf315e-115","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/replay.d.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":821,"output_tokens":3123,"cache_read_input_tokens":170880,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"b5d49474-8ce4-419c-bccd-dbdbdc30fb2f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-5c20ed04-1d36-4a41-844c-c3ba6cdf315e-115","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * Recordings of play (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). From a level's start, its seed and the\\n * input of every frame decide everything that happens in it, so a recording is just those: the\\n * engine keeps one for the level that is running (`engine.recording()`), `engine.replay()` plays\\n * one back frame for frame, and `engine.fingerprint()` hashes the state, so two runs that\\n * should match can be compared. A recorded bug is a file; replayed, it is a test.\\n *\\n *   const rec = engine.recording();                          // JSON: seed, game, frames\\n *   await engine.replay(rec, () => engine.loadGame(new MyGame()));\\n10→ *   engine.fingerprint() === fingerprintAtTheEnd;            // the same game, frame for frame\\n *\\n * What a replay can't reproduce: anything a game takes from outside `ctx.input` and its frames\\n * (DOM events it listens to itself, a model that finishes loading mid-play, the wall clock, a\\n * camera left turned by the level before). Data a game must take from the page (a save in\\n * storage, a text field) goes through `ctx.input.send()`, which the recording keeps.\\n * docs/engine/determinism.md *Recording and replay*.\\n */\\nimport type { Viewport } from './framing';\\nimport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\n20→import { type Snapshot } from './snapshot';\\nexport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\nexport interface Recording {\\n    readonly format: 1;\\n    /** The engine version that recorded it (a different one may not replay the same). */\\n    readonly engine: string;\\n    /** The game's name when it was recorded. */\\n    readonly game: string;\\n    /** What the game was made with (`Game.args`), when its name alone can't rebuild it. */\\n    readonly args?: unknown;\\n30→    /** The engine seed (`ctx.random` comes from it and the game's name). */\\n    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Game time when the level began (`ctx.time` carries on across levels). */\\n    readonly time: number;\\n    /** Every frame the level ran, from its first. */\\n    readonly frames: readonly InputFrame[];\\n    /** True when the level ran longer than a recording keeps (the frames are its start). */\\n    readonly truncated?: boolean;\\n    /** The save-state the frames start from (`engine.restore()` began this recording), if any. */\\n    readonly from?: Snapshot;\\n40→    /**\\n     * The viewport it was recorded on, as it was when the level began (CSS pixels and DPR): the art\\n     * resolution, pointer aim and the HUD's layout follow it. A headless replay frames for it; a page\\n     * warns when its own differs. A page resized during the level is not followed.\\n     */\\n    readonly view?: Viewport;\\n}\\n/** Frames a recording keeps: an hour at 60 fps. */\\nexport declare const MAX_FRAMES = 216000;\\n/** Is `v` a recording this engine can replay? Returns the problem, or null. */\\n50→export declare function recordingProblem(v: unknown): string | null;\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * Recordings of play (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). From a level's start, its seed and the\\n * input of every frame decide everything that happens in it, so a recording is just those: the\\n * engine keeps one for the level that is running (`engine.recording()`), `engine.replay()` plays\\n * one back frame for frame, and `engine.fingerprint()` hashes the state, so two runs that\\n * should match can be compared. A recorded bug is a file; replayed, it is a test.\\n *\\n *   const rec = engine.recording();                          // JSON: seed, game, frames\\n *   await engine.replay(rec, () => engine.loadGame(new MyGame()));\\n10→ *   engine.fingerprint() === fingerprintAtTheEnd;            // the same game, frame for frame\\n *\\n * What a replay can't reproduce: anything a game takes from outside `ctx.input` and its frames\\n * (DOM events it listens to itself, a model that finishes loading mid-play, the wall clock, a\\n * camera left turned by the level before). Data a game must take from the page (a save in\\n * storage, a text field) goes through `ctx.input.send()`, which the recording keeps.\\n * docs/engine/determinism.md *Recording and replay*.\\n */\\nimport type { Viewport } from './framing';\\nimport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\n20→import { type Snapshot } from './snapshot';\\nexport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\nexport interface Recording {\\n    readonly format: 1;\\n    /** The engine version that recorded it (a different one may not replay the same). */\\n    readonly engine: string;\\n    /** The game's name when it was recorded. */\\n    readonly game: string;\\n    /** What the game was made with (`Game.args`), when its name alone can't rebuild it. */\\n    readonly args?: unknown;\\n30→    /** The engine seed (`ctx.random` comes from it and the game's name). */\\n    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Game time when the level began (`ctx.time` carries on across levels). */\\n    readonly time: number;\\n    /** Every frame the level ran, from its first. */\\n    readonly frames: readonly InputFrame[];\\n    /** True when the level ran longer than a recording keeps (the frames are its start). */\\n    readonly truncated?: boolean;\\n    /** The save-state the frames start from (`engine.restore()` began this recording), if any. */\\n    readonly from?: Snapshot;\\n40→    /**\\n     * The viewport it was recorded on, as it was when the level began (CSS pixels and DPR): the art\\n     * resolution, pointer aim and the HUD's layout follow it. A headless replay frames for it; a page\\n     * warns when its own differs. A page resized during the level is not followed.\\n     */\\n    readonly view?: Viewport;\\n}\\n/** Frames a recording keeps: an hour at 60 fps. */\\nexport declare const MAX_FRAMES = 216000;\\n/** Is `v` a recording this engine can replay? Returns the problem, or null. */\\n50→export declare function recordingProblem(v: unknown): string | null;\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/replay.d.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * Recordings of play (docs/DOCTRINE.md, principle 1). From a level's start, its seed and the\\n * input of every frame decide everything that happens in it, so a recording is just those: the\\n * engine keeps one for the level that is running (`engine.recording()`), `engine.replay()` plays\\n * one back frame for frame, and `engine.fingerprint()` hashes the state, so two runs that\\n * should match can be compared. A recorded bug is a file; replayed, it is a test.\\n *\\n *   const rec = engine.recording();                          // JSON: seed, game, frames\\n *   await engine.replay(rec, () => engine.loadGame(new MyGame()));\\n *   engine.fingerprint() === fingerprintAtTheEnd;            // the same game, frame for frame\\n *\\n * What a replay can't reproduce: anything a game takes from outside `ctx.input` and its frames\\n * (DOM events it listens to itself, a model that finishes loading mid-play, the wall clock, a\\n * camera left turned by the level before). Data a game must take from the page (a save in\\n * storage, a text field) goes through `ctx.input.send()`, which the recording keeps.\\n * docs/engine/determinism.md *Recording and replay*.\\n */\\nimport type { Viewport } from './framing';\\nimport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\nimport { type Snapshot } from './snapshot';\\nexport type { InputFrame } from './input';\\nexport interface Recording {\\n    readonly format: 1;\\n    /** The engine version that recorded it (a different one may not replay the same). */\\n    readonly engine: string;\\n    /** The game's name when it was recorded. */\\n    readonly game: string;\\n    /** What the game was made with (`Game.args`), when its name alone can't rebuild it. */\\n    readonly args?: unknown;\\n    /** The engine seed (`ctx.random` comes from it and the game's name). */\\n    readonly seed: number;\\n    /** Game time when the level began (`ctx.time` carries on across levels). */\\n    readonly time: number;\\n    /** Every frame the level ran, from its first. */\\n    readonly frames: readonly InputFrame[];\\n    /** True when the level ran longer than a recording keeps (the frames are its start). */\\n    readonly truncated?: boolean;\\n    /** The save-state the frames start from (`engine.restore()` began this recording), if any. */\\n    readonly from?: Snapshot;\\n    /**\\n     * The viewport it was recorded on, as it was when the level began (CSS pixels and DPR): the art\\n     * resolution, pointer aim and the HUD's layout follow it. A headless replay frames for it; a page\\n     * warns when its own differs. A page resized during the level is not followed.\\n     */\\n    readonly view?: Viewport;\\n}\\n/** Frames a recording keeps: an hour at 60 fps. */\\nexport declare const MAX_FRAMES = 216000;\\n/** Is `v` a recording this engine can replay? Returns the problem, or null. */\\nexport declare function recordingProblem(v: unknown): string | null;\\n\",\"total_lines\":50}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"d32ae9c6-071c-464c-8af0-3fc46ae8622a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_158","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A replay cannot reproduce anything that a game takes from outside its context.\n\nStorage and guest recreation via keys are safe for replay. setDriver is excluded from input. Dynamic voice imports are harmless since speech does not alter simulation state. Date.now is absent from fingerprints. Randomness is controlled by the forked dot implementation.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-808e73c7-eb45-4a7b-bf9e-b975e93bf836-116","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"fingerprint\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/Engine.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3869,"output_tokens":367,"cache_read_input_tokens":171648,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"b408a0bc-8474-4c88-950a-7a4ccc5dfe34"}
