{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-6135c8f4-a5e3-4c3c-bb1d-f2cf02f81bc9-117","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"80→     * Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before\\n     * `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here\\n     * (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is\\n     * reported like any hook's (`engine.errors`, on screen); the game runs on.\\n     */\\n    ready?(ctx: GameContext): void;\\n    /** Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state). */\\n    update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void;\\n    /** World position the camera follows. */\\n    cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n90→    /** First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up). */\\n    eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n    /** One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...). */\\n    status?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives\\n     * outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random\\n     * streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay must\\n     * reproduce: no timings or measurements.\\n     */\\n100→    fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a\\n     * later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the\\n     * handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\\n     */\\n    snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown;\\n    /**\\n     * Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already\\n     * set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\\n110→     */\\n    restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode\\n     * with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks\\n     * the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `restore`.\\n     */\\n    beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world,\",\"content_concise\":\"80→     * Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before\\n     * `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here\\n     * (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is\\n     * reported like any hook's (`engine.errors`, on screen); the game runs on.\\n     */\\n    ready?(ctx: GameContext): void;\\n    /** Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state). */\\n    update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void;\\n    /** World position the camera follows. */\\n    cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n90→    /** First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up). */\\n    eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n    /** One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...). */\\n    status?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives\\n     * outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random\\n     * streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay must\\n     * reproduce: no timings or measurements.\\n     */\\n100→    fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a\\n     * later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the\\n     * handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\\n     */\\n    snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown;\\n    /**\\n     * Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already\\n     * set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\\n110→     */\\n    restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode\\n     * with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks\\n     * the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `restore`.\\n     */\\n    beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world,\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/Engine.d.ts\",\"offset\":80,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"     * Runs when the engine starts running this game: after `Engine.start` (just before\\n     * `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`. Install page handles for tools here\\n     * (`window.__MYGAME__`), so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception is\\n     * reported like any hook's (`engine.errors`, on screen); the game runs on.\\n     */\\n    ready?(ctx: GameContext): void;\\n    /** Runs once per rendered frame after physics (animation, pickups, UI state). */\\n    update?(ctx: GameContext, dt: number): void;\\n    /** World position the camera follows. */\\n    cameraTarget(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n    /** First-person eye position (defaults to cameraTarget + 0.7 up). */\\n    eyePosition?(ctx: GameContext): Vector3;\\n    /** One-line status for the debug UI (score, lives...). */\\n    status?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as a short string or hash, for `engine.fingerprint()`: what lives\\n     * outside physics and `ctx.random` (positions, life, counters, the game's own random\\n     * streams), so two runs that should match (a replay) can be compared. Only what a replay must\\n     * reproduce: no timings or measurements.\\n     */\\n    fingerprint?(ctx: GameContext): string;\\n    /**\\n     * The game's own state as plain data, between frames (`engine.snapshot()`): everything a\\n     * later frame reads that the engine does not keep (its entities, timers, random streams; the\\n     * handles of its physics bodies). With `restore`, the game has save-states.\\n     */\\n    snapshot?(ctx: GameContext): unknown;\\n    /**\\n     * Put the game back to what `snapshot` returned (`engine.restore()`): the same game, already\\n     * set up. Find physics bodies again by handle (`ctx.physics.world.getRigidBody(h)`).\\n     */\\n    restore?(ctx: GameContext, state: unknown): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Optional: leave whatever mode the game is in before a restore changes anything (a mode\\n     * with a camera of its own, a dialog, a level): `engine.restore()` calls it first, then checks\\n     * the snapshot fits (the camera preset) and puts the engine's part back, then `restore`.\\n     */\\n    beforeRestore?(ctx: GameContext): void | Promise<void>;\\n    /**\\n     * Called after `engine.setCamera()` swapped the camera preset mid-game. The world,\\n\",\"total_lines\":613}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"0b691ff6-0ddc-4b98-bb0b-fe6d41fb0b67"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_160","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"World.fingerprint(ctx) should return app.\n\nAdding fingerprint to World via search_replace. The cameraTarget(ctx) signature differs from Engine but is acceptable in JS. Preparing the big test and will reuse the title app as guest for the activity star case. Will run --fast with a heartbeat to check step performance. The first engine boot succeeded; the test will boot again.\n\nDesigning a dev slider test. The plan is to pause an activity, adjust a shared tunable, resume, and step frames to verify the simulation reads the new value. A sampleTravel helper will measure dot movement across 60 frames to confirm the behavior change.\n\nThe dev slider test will verify the live link by confirming `app.sim.T` and `app.tunables` are the same object. After nudging a slider, the test will step frames to ensure the value does not snap back. It will also compare dot position variance between default and maximum noise settings.\n\nResetting and replaying with different seeds makes variance comparisons unreliable. The simpler test nudges a slider, steps once, and asserts the shared object identity. To verify live simulation behavior, timeScale will be raised to 1 while in dev mode so the activity updates and the dot moves more than under default settings.\n\nComparing dot movement across different seeds is unreliable because the fork increments them. Resetting the dot state to test noise directly would violate encapsulation. The acceptance criteria require live slider behavior plus both reset paths.\n\nThe dev slider test will assert that nudging a slider immediately changes the value the simulation reads, and that reset restores defaults. Reset-all will restore every key across tabs. A counter will verify that a tunable change is visible during the next fixed step.\n\nThe dev slider test will also step one frame with timeScale 1 while the activity is running, not paused. The tools test will verify that jump starts ACTIVITIES[1] because jumpPick increments before the activity begins, and finish sets stars to 1 after incrementing finishPick. Both tools will be tested on a running simulation.\n\nThe tools test will run through the full toolchain on a fresh app: pause, enter dev mode, switch to tools, and exercise day, drips, unlock, overlay, hud, god, time, line, anim, sound, export, and auto. Wipe clears the guest but leaves dev mode active. The sound tool will be verified by asserting engine.audio in headless mode. Focus resets correctly after the more button expands rows.\n\nThe sound tool will be verified by asserting that counts.chime increments after the first press. Export will be checked by confirming the exported string is set. The auto tool will be tested by verifying that the first press sets skillPick to 1 and driver to 'idle'. A concern was raised about the time tool: setting timeScale to 0.25 while still in dev mode could unpause the simulation and cause the activity to finish mid-test.\n\nThe time tool will be tested last, immediately before jump. Because the available scales are 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, and 3, none of them is zero, so the tool always unpause the simulation. The test will accept that the activity may advance during this check.\n\nThe time tool test will assert the scale change and then manually set engine.timeScale back to zero to keep the simulation stable. The god toggle does not finish the activity. Overlay and hud are simple boolean flags. The day tool increments guest.day modulo seven and resets today. Drips adds fifty items. Unlock sets unlockAll. The camera tool sets free mode, which may throw in headless but is caught by runTool.\n\nThe camera tool sets freeCam to true even if setCamera throws, so the test can assert that flag directly. The anim tool sets viewerClip, and the line tool sets dialogue while clearing spoken. World.fingerprint will be added to the test suite. analyzeClip will be tested by booting a second headless engine with the hero loaded, or by loading the hero during the first boot.\n\nThe analyzeClip test will run in a separate short engine boot so hero loading does not block gameplay tests. It will be placed at the end of the suite. A noHero flag avoids reloading the hero during that boot.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dcbbee75-13ba-4972-a2ce-e10fe2920302-118","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/scenes/world.js","old_string":"  cameraTarget() {\n    return this.target.set(0, 1, 0);\n  }","new_string":"  cameraTarget() {\n    return this.target.set(0, 1, 0);\n  }\n\n  fingerprint() {\n    return this.app.fingerprint();\n  }"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-dcbbee75-13ba-4972-a2ce-e10fe2920302-119","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607\" && node --input-type=module -e '\nimport { Engine, analyzeClip, compileClip, HERO_RIG, validateClip, validateTexture, checkModel } from \"./pixel-engine.js\";\nimport { App } from \"./src/app.js\";\nimport { World } from \"./src/scenes/world.js\";\nimport { CLIPS } from \"./src/art/clips.js\";\nimport { clinicTile } from \"./src/art/textures.js\";\nimport { MODELS } from \"./src/art/models.js\";\nconst tex = validateTexture(clinicTile);\nconsole.log(\"tex\", tex.problems, tex.warnings);\nfor (const m of MODELS) {\n  const c = checkModel(m);\n  console.log(\"model\", m.id, \"p\", (c.problems||[]).length, \"w\", (c.warnings||[]).length);\n}\nconst app = new App({ storage: null });\nconst world = new World(app);\nconst engine = await Engine.headless(world, { viewport: { width: 320, height: 180 }, camera: { preset: \"fixed\", position: [9,7,11], target: [0,1,0] } });\nconsole.log(\"