,115,32,99,114,101,97,116,101,100,41,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\nTree v 0 p 0 \\nHeron v 0 p 0 \\nWarrior v 0 p 0 \\nChair v 0 p 0 \\nFold v 0 p 0 \\nPlank v 0 p 0 \\nSquatRep v 0 p 0 \\nLungeRep v 0 p 0 \\nPress v 0 p 0 \\nStepL v 0 p 0 \\nStepR v 0 p 0 \\n(node:4067641) Warning: The 'NO_COLOR' env is ignored due to the 'FORCE_COLOR' env being set.\\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"cd \\\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607\\\" && node --input-type=module -e '\\nimport { Engine, analyzeClip, compileClips, HERO_RIG, validateClip } from \\\"./pixel-engine.js\\\";\\nimport { App } from \\\"./src/app.js\\\";\\nimport { World } from \\\"./src/scenes/world.js\\\";\\nimport { CLIPS } from \\\"./src/art/clips.js\\\";\\nconst app = new App({ storage: null });\\nconst world = new World(app);\\nawait Engine.headless(world, { viewport: { width: 320, height: 180 }, camera: { preset: \\\"fixed\\\", position: [9,7,11], target: [0,1,0] } });\\nconst model = world.heroModel;\\nfor (const def of CLIPS) {\\n  const v = validateClip(def, HERO_RIG);\\n  const clip = compileClips([def], HERO_RIG, model)[0];\\n  const report = analyzeClip(model, HERO_RIG, def, clip);\\n  const probs = report.problems || [];\\n  console.log(def.name, \\\"v\\\", v.length, \\\"p\\\", probs.length, probs.slice(0, 3).join(\\\" || \\\"));\\n}\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Re-run analyzeClip with compileClips\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3%2Fwork/01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469/terminal/call-c825021d-70af-4339-935d-3d17bc965c14-126.log\",\"total_bytes\":557}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"3478f738-b102-41db-abc0-7c85e2c53a8a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_166","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Clips are clean. Now writing the big test file.\n\nWorld.compileClips is already correct. The test will use mulberry32 seeds for dot bands and include a quick timing check. I'll trust lines.js allLineStrings and count.\n\nThe Nintendo regex blocks \"Wii Fit\" but allows \"Fit\". The test will be split into node tests and a browser module. The week test failure may be caused by menu focus.\n\nchoose() will throw with a list of available rows. Island navigation returns to page 0 on back, not the menu. Drafting playDay and sleepDay helpers to automate island activities and rest.\n\nDay 7 sleep triggers confession instead of weigh. The endDay helper now handles that branch by advancing through confession and onward to week 2 weigh. It then waits for the question screen, selects the first answer, and proceeds onward.\n\nstartWeigh is synchronous, so the game is already in weigh or confession mode immediately after sleep. waitMode steps the engine in 30-frame chunks until it reaches the target mode. A 14-second question timeout equals 840 frames, and 12000 frames covers longstand and outro safely.\n\nThe Day 1 test sequence starts from the title screen, selects week, slot0, done, and board, then waits for the question and answers. setDriver('expert') must run before activities so weigh and activities autopilot, but menus still require manual key presses. Expert mode skips intros for both weigh and activities.\n\nDay 4 plays are tree, squat, and hoop. Hoop lasts 60 seconds.\n\nA visit helper navigates to a station, checks for a back button, then returns. Day 1 visits the plaza and beach. Day 2 unlocks the lagoon by visiting it and confirming the act-header row appears before playing lunge.\n\nDay 1 lagoon should not show an act-header. Hard mode on day 6 is covered by the star sweep with guest.hard enabled, so it does not need a separate week requirement. The test will assert the drips formula for every activity in the sweep and for the week jar. A shared memory map will serve as the storage object for mid-week saves.\n\nThe memory storage map is now fully defined with getItem, setItem, and removeItem. Tests will verify that corrupted JSON fails gracefully, missing storage falls back to in-memory persistence, and reload restores the saved guest data.\n\nThe reload test now verifies that a persisted guest named Ada with day 4 and 30 drips survives a fresh App instance. A storage object that throws on getItem and setItem still falls back to in-memory persistence. The engine pause test is starting.\n\nThe engine pause test is now fully drafted. It steps the engine, pauses with KeyP, verifies timeScale hits zero and playT freezes, navigates to resume using arrow keys, resumes, and confirms the simulation resumes advancing.\n\nThe pause test is confirmed safe because activities are pausable. A fresh app instance will be used for the attract test to avoid mode conflicts from a paused state. The full attract loop spans ten items and lasts about 140 seconds, which fits within the engine's frame budget.\n\nbeginAttract now shallow-copies tunables so ringScale doubling does not mutate the original. The dot band test needs no engine. I will write the test file and browser.mjs next, emitting only failures to scratch. The full medley must still run.\n\nThe --fast flag includes the medley test because it runs in Node. The full week simulation totals roughly 33,000 frames, or about 551 seconds. That duration is acceptable.\n\nThe sloppy skill test will run alongside expert. The full sweep totals roughly 144,000 frames at 1 ms per frame, or about 29 seconds at 0.2 ms per frame. Engines will be disposed before the browser module to prevent leaks.\n\nBrowser tests will be written to be resilient. Screenshots will be saved under scratch/shots. The settings button selector is `.settings-button`. If the phone touchOnly test fails, `detectTouchOnly` will be updated. Playwright's `isMobile` flag might not reach Chromium media queries in all versions, so that behavior needs verification.\n\ndetectTouchOnly will be tightened to match the spec exactly: `hover: none` and `pointer: coarse` with no gamepad. If Playwright omits those media queries, the failure output will reveal the exact values needed for a compatibility fix. Chromium headless with `isMobile` already sets `pointer: coarse`. The engine writes the unavailable message into the page.\n\nThe browser harness will be served from scratch/hubserve with game.html, harness.html, bridge.js, and round.js. Playwright will navigate to the harness URL and wait for dataset.complete.\n\nThe browser test timeout is raised to 120 seconds to accommodate four players with latency. The harness page title will not be checked; instead, the game iframe title must read \"Mint Dial\". This check runs before waiting for dataset.complete.\n\nThe browser test will load game.html directly when no session exists, verifying the title screen and inactive AIHub state. HTML meta tags will be checked via node tests against the built file. The test runner runs all node tests first, then builds and runs browser, hub, and screenshot tests only if the fast flag is absent; a node failure stops execution immediately.\n\nA node test will verify that the about pages include medical, source, feedback, and kit credits. The exit interview shape must match after build.\n\nThe exit interview shape will be verified by importing the function directly in node tests, and the built file will be checked after a full build. The World fingerprint method was added, and cameraTarget no longer takes a context argument. compileClips is confirmed safe because prior hero and no-hero boots both succeeded.\n\nThe test helpers will be placed at the top of the file. A tap is simulated by setting a key to true, stepping the engine, then setting it false and stepping again; the press registers on the first step. On the title screen with a single row, pressing ArrowDown wraps focus back to the first option. The name menu row updates as text is typed, but the test only needs to press Enter on the done button. A potential bug where PRESS includes MouseLeft and Space was noted, though it does not affect keyboard-only tests.\n\nKeyP is confirmed safe during activities because it only triggers the pause menu in idle mode. The dev tab order is Balance, Breath and reps, Rhythm, Games, Economy, then Tools. KeyE cycles through these tabs. ArrowRight nudges sliders via menuInput. The reset-tab row is selected with Enter.\n\nA `focus(id)` helper will arrow down until the active row matches the target. Each dev tab is split across two pages: the first five keys appear on page zero, and the rest on page one. The test will switch tabs with KeyE, then page through rows while tracking seen keys in a Set.\n\nThe dev tab test iterates rows, focuses each tunable key, taps ArrowRight, and asserts the value changed and synced to the simulation. It pages through \"more\" when needed and fails if any key is missed.\n\nThe dev tab test will nudge sliders across multiple tabs, then run reset-all to verify all values return to defaults. Reset-tab will be tested separately to confirm it restores the active tab. Because timeScale is zero during development, nudging sliders cannot trigger activity completion. The time tool will be left until after slider changes are finished.\n\nThe medley test will run four bots with a ghost lane. It steps the engine in chunks of 120 frames and checks window states at t=10, 60, 70, 100, 125, 140, and 180. The ghost dot.t must remain zero throughout. The simulation object is confirmed to hold tunables under sim.T. The family hour node test and the full medley are ready to be written.\n\nThe medley test will step the engine in 60-frame chunks and record the running time. Each step(1) equals one fixed frame at 1/60 s. The ghost hide check pushes a message while the window index is zero, then verifies the dot hides after presentation.\n\nThe ghost lane will be the only lane with simulated false. The test file and browser harness will be written as test.mjs and browser.mjs, with screenshots kept inside the browser module. A node test helper is being drafted to import Engine, App, World, and CAMERA from their source paths.\n\nThe `boot` helper is now fully drafted. It creates a new `App` with `noHero: true`, then either instantiates a headless `Engine` or loads the existing one with a fresh `World`. The first call creates the engine and steps it once; later calls reuse the same instance. The `noHero` flag is set before `World` reads it, so the hero is correctly skipped.\n\nThe `boot` helper will reuse the same camera when loading a new `World`. The outer `engine` variable is correctly scoped for both headless creation and `loadGame` reuse. Static imports at the top of `test.mjs` are safe because the script runs from the dev folder, so relative paths like `../pixel-engine.js` resolve properly.\n\nPaths will be resolved relative to `import.meta.url` so the working directory does not matter. A `beat()` helper will be added for timing checks. The browser harness will export `runBrowserSuite(ctx)` where `ctx` holds the HTML path, scratch folder, and a fail function; errors will be thrown and caught by `test.mjs`. Screenshots will be written from data URLs by splitting on the comma and decoding base64 into a buffer.\n\nA blank screenshot capture is treated as a test failure. If the browser capture throws an environmental error, the stack trace is saved to a scratch file and the test proceeds. A `captured` flag tracks whether the screenshot was successfully taken.\n\nA `sig` helper will serialize game state for comparison across boots. Music must be exactly 0.10, so numeric tolerance is used instead of JSON string comparison. Page errors from the NO_COLOR variable are ignored.\n\nThe browser harness will fail on page errors and log console output to browser.log. Console errors will be treated as failures unless they are known engine warnings. Strict pageerror handling will be relaxed after the first run so that expected engine messages do not block success.\n\nThe App constructor will accept a `noHero` option. The boot helper will pass it directly to the constructor instead of setting the property afterward.\n\nThe constructor will set `this.noHero` directly from the option for cleanliness. Session games still skip the hero because `World.setup` checks `!this.app.session`. The test suite is now ready to be written.\n\nThe engine's `__GAME__` reference updates during `loadGame`, so an explicit `step(1)` will be added after each boot to ensure the new world is fully drawn. The headless viewport shape was already validated in the smoke test. The entire test file will be written in one pass, and a `PROGRESS` helper will be added later.\n\nThe dev tools test will start an activity, step the engine so the fixed runs, then press KeyP to open the dev menu. Because timeScale is zero, stepping still updates the simulation and taps remain valid. The choose() helper will skip ArrowDown if the target row is already focused, then press Enter. A tap() helper is being drafted to briefly press a key, step one frame, and release it.\n\nThe `choose` helper will try up to 12 rows to find the target, then press Enter. The browser harness will be kept compact. A glyph test flow is being drafted for a booted page, starting with a gamepad stub that has button 0 unpressed.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3edb8536-4422-45ab-a4ac-3a0e83b137ff-127","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/input.d.ts","offset":218,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3edb8536-4422-45ab-a4ac-3a0e83b137ff-128","name":"search_replace","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/app.js","old_string":"    this.session = opts.session || null;\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;","new_string":"    this.session = opts.session || null;\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;\n    this.noHero = !!opts.noHero;"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":703,"output_tokens":6650,"cache_read_input_tokens":192896,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"b3213404-1b2e-4311-a3e0-5a0445a27f41"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3edb8536-4422-45ab-a4ac-3a0e83b137ff-127","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"218→    get queuedPresses(): string[];\\n    /** Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input). */\\n220→    clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void;\\n    /**\\n     * -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left\\n     * stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\\n     */\\n    moveAxis(): {\\n        x: number;\\n        y: number;\\n    };\\n    /** Call at the end of each rendered frame. */\\n230→    endFrame(): void;\\n    /** Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false). */\\n    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not. */\\n    setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would. */\\n    setButtons(bits: number): void;\\n    /** Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`. */\\n    get mouseButtons(): number;\\n    /** A pointer button (a key code) goes down (pressed, unless held already) or up, where the pointer is. */\\n240→    private button;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer\\n     * was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where\\n     * it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play reads `isDown`.\\n     */\\n    get buttonEvents(): readonly ButtonEvent[];\\n    /**\\n     * Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked,\\n     * a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It\\n250→     * arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keeps it there, so\\n     * a replay delivers the same data at the same frame (and ignores what is sent live). It goes\\n     * through JSON first: live play reads exactly what a replay will.\\n     */\\n    send(command: unknown): void;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered\\n     * frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\",\"content_concise\":\"218→    get queuedPresses(): string[];\\n    /** Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input). */\\n220→    clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void;\\n    /**\\n     * -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left\\n     * stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\\n     */\\n    moveAxis(): {\\n        x: number;\\n        y: number;\\n    };\\n    /** Call at the end of each rendered frame. */\\n230→    endFrame(): void;\\n    /** Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false). */\\n    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not. */\\n    setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would. */\\n    setButtons(bits: number): void;\\n    /** Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`. */\\n    get mouseButtons(): number;\\n    /** A pointer button (a key code) goes down (pressed, unless held already) or up, where the pointer is. */\\n240→    private button;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer\\n     * was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where\\n     * it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play reads `isDown`.\\n     */\\n    get buttonEvents(): readonly ButtonEvent[];\\n    /**\\n     * Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked,\\n     * a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It\\n250→     * arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keeps it there, so\\n     * a replay delivers the same data at the same frame (and ignores what is sent live). It goes\\n     * through JSON first: live play reads exactly what a replay will.\\n     */\\n    send(command: unknown): void;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered\\n     * frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/input.d.ts\",\"offset\":218,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"    get queuedPresses(): string[];\\n    /** Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input). */\\n    clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void;\\n    /**\\n     * -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left\\n     * stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\\n     */\\n    moveAxis(): {\\n        x: number;\\n        y: number;\\n    };\\n    /** Call at the end of each rendered frame. */\\n    endFrame(): void;\\n    /** Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false). */\\n    setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not. */\\n    setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void;\\n    /** Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would. */\\n    setButtons(bits: number): void;\\n    /** Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`. */\\n    get mouseButtons(): number;\\n    /** A pointer button (a key code) goes down (pressed, unless held already) or up, where the pointer is. */\\n    private button;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's pointer button presses and releases, in order, each with where the pointer\\n     * was: a drag shorter than a frame (a script's) still starts where it started and ends where\\n     * it ended. Recorded with the frame. UI that acts on clicks reads these; play reads `isDown`.\\n     */\\n    get buttonEvents(): readonly ButtonEvent[];\\n    /**\\n     * Hand the game data from outside the canvas: a DOM widget's value, a file the player picked,\\n     * a save read from storage, anything a game would otherwise take straight from the page. It\\n     * arrives in `commands` at the next frame the game runs, and the recording keeps it there, so\\n     * a replay delivers the same data at the same frame (and ignores what is sent live). It goes\\n     * through JSON first: live play reads exactly what a replay will.\\n     */\\n    send(command: unknown): void;\\n    /**\\n     * This frame's commands (`send`), oldest first; empty on most frames. They last one rendered\\n     * frame: read them in `update` (`fixedUpdate` runs zero or more times a frame).\\n\",\"total_lines\":278}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3edb8536-4422-45ab-a4ac-3a0e83b137ff-128","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"    this.session = opts.session || null;\\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;\",\"new_string\":\"    this.session = opts.session || null;\\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;\\n    this.noHero = !!opts.noHero;\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/app.js has been updated successfully.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/app.js has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/app.js\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"    this.session = opts.session || null;\\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;\",\"old_line\":109,\"new_string\":\"    this.session = opts.session || null;\\n    this.touchOnly = !!opts.touchOnly;\\n    this.noHero = !!opts.noHero;\",\"new_line\":109,\"context_before\":\"    };\\n    if (disk.settings) Object.assign(this.settings, disk.settings);\\n    if (!disk.settings) this.settings.music = 0.1;\\n\",\"context_after\":\"    this.mode = this.session ? 'card' : 'title';\\n    this.device = new Device();\\n    this.local = new LocalDriver();\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"9e6f507c-a4a6-480c-bb68-1833ca8bf86a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_167","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"setPointer uses normalized device coordinates.\n\nHover converts normalized device coordinates to HUD pixels. Widget hits convert widget center back to normalized device coordinates. setPointer updates the pointer position and mouseDelta for the next frame.\n\nThe device threshold is 2 pixels. setPointer may not generate enough mouseDelta to trigger the switch. Pressing MouseLeft also forces the mouse prompt.\n\ndevice.update runs before menu logic. A click will activate the menu once the device switches to mouse. I'll test this by calling setPointer twice with large offsets to force a mouseDelta above 2 pixels. If that fails, a single large jump should trigger the switch. The threshold is correct if mouseDelta is measured in CSS pixels.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-162d6397-da36-4fe2-bb60-cd522925fe4a-129","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607\" && node --input-type=module -e '\nimport { Dot, DOT_DEFAULTS } from \"./src/core/dot.js\";\nimport { BotDriver } from \"./src/core/drivers.js\";\nimport { mulberry32 } from \"./src/core/rng.js\";\nfunction band(skill, seed) {\n  const dot = new Dot(mulberry32(seed));\n  const bot = new BotDriver(skill, mulberry32(seed + 99));\n  const dt = 1/60;\n  let inside = 0, n = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < 37*60; i++) {\n    const u = skill === \"idle\" ? {x:0,y:0} : bot.read(dot, dt);\n    dot.step(dt, u.x||0, u.y||0, DOT_DEFAULTS, {});\n    if (i >= 120) { n++; if (Math.hypot(dot.x, dot.y) < 0.3) inside++; }\n  }\n  return inside / n;\n}\nfor (const seed of [1, 2, 7, 20, 99]) {\n  const row = [\"idle\",\"sloppy\",\"average\",\"good\",\"expert\"].map(s => s+\" \"+(band(s, seed)*100).toFixed(1));\n  console.log(seed, row.join(\" | \"));\n}\n'","description":"Measure Dot ring-time bands for a few seeds"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7386,"output_tokens":1345,"cache_read_input_tokens":193536,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"51304838-42a0-4981-82da-581de6141890"}
