4,32,57,41,59,10,49,53,54,53,58,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,108,105,110,101,40,118,105,101,119,44,32,105,100,44,32,116,101,120,116,44,32,121,44,32,115,99,97,108,101,44,32,99,111,108,111,114,41,32,123,10,49,53,55,51,58,32,32,97,112,112,46,119,105,100,103,101,116,40,105,100,44,32,120,44,32,121,44,32,77,97,116,104,46,109,105,110,40,119,105,100,116,104,44,32,119,32,45,32,120,41,44,32,57,32,42,32,115,99,97,108,101,41,59,10,49,53,56,51,58,32,32,97,112,112,46,119,105,100,103,101,116,40,39,115,116,97,114,115,39,44,32,120,44,32,121,44,32,50,56,44,32,55,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":7,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/src/app.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":1339,\"content\":\"  widget(id, x, y, w, h) {\"},{\"line_number\":1451,\"content\":\"    app.widget('band', 0, 0, w, Math.min(h, 78));\"},{\"line_number\":1548,\"content\":\"      app.widget(`row-${row.id}`, x, ry, Math.min(width, w - m * 2), 9 * rowScale);\"},{\"line_number\":1556,\"content\":\"    app.widget('prompt', m, py, measureText(prompt, 1).width, 9);\"},{\"line_number\":1565,\"content\":\"function line(view, id, text, y, scale, color) {\"},{\"line_number\":1573,\"content\":\"  app.widget(id, x, y, Math.min(width, w - x), 9 * scale);\"},{\"line_number\":1583,\"content\":\"  app.widget('stars', x, y, 28, 7);\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"80fa27d1-b333-4887-89a0-bbcf4b6b5f74"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_196","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hover widgets begin with the prefix \"row-\".\n\nHover requires a mouse device. Browser hover will use the same math as the game. Clicking activates the focused row. Hovering the start row and clicking should switch mode to menu.\n\nBoot 1 will capture UI state and close. Boot 2 will compare states before interacting. Widgets must fit inside the HUD bounds. HUD dimensions are not included in state.\n\nHUD dimensions are available via `app.ctx.hud.width` and height. Widgets must stay within those bounds. Page overflow will be checked against `scrollWidth`. The test code will be split into logical functions in one file. A ten-tap limit for the down arrow is safer than eight.\n\nEnter confirms the name screen, while KeyE does not. Backspace is avoided to prevent editing the name. Most screens start with focus on the first row. The settings test is excluded from the week menu. Island's \"more\" option is the final row. A potential bug was noted: `choose` might not function correctly when the mode is activity.\n\nA single `engine.step(120)` call advances the fixed timestep by two seconds. The 14-second weigh test equals 840 frames, capped at 1000. Frame delta time is confirmed to be one-sixtieth. Verbose comments will be avoided.\n\nGhost lane dots start with zero time and must not advance during simulation. The hidden property is set after the first fixed step in tickMedley. Ghost lanes are excluded from simulated state, and their step count remains zero.\n\nA family hour test will run before the long medley to catch a broken join quickly. The `getGamepads` stub must be assigned to `globalThis.navigator`. The pad button press state must not be set true before the first step, or the rising edge detection will fail. Joining from the menu requires clearing the driver and starting from the title screen.\n\nA `boot(engine, storage)` helper will reset the engine between major test sections to prevent state leaks. Tests will run sequentially on one engine instance. `loadGame` rebuilds the model but skips the hero GLB, so it is fast enough. The clips engine loads the hero GLB and should be disposed immediately. `checkModel`, `parseSong`, and `validateTexture` need no engine. `analyzeClip` requires a loaded model in a world.\n\nThe hero model loads correctly. The dress hides eyes and analyzeClip shows zero problems. Browser tests will use shot.mjs as an export. PNGs from data URLs will be decoded with base64 and written to disk. Luminance values will be returned as numbers in the page to avoid decoding PNGs in Node. Capture throws will be caught.\n\nCapture errors will be logged to capture-error.txt and the test will proceed with state-only. If capture succeeds but luminance is below eight, the test fails. The HUD will be captured as a composite image when possible; if that throws, the raw capture becomes the fallback. The pixel array will be converted to a Uint8ClampedArray for ImageData.\n\nThe scene background is set in the world so capture shows sky and ground. Quality high is already active outside sessions. The week, sweep, and medley tests might exceed ten minutes, so a fast path will be tried first. The four-bot medley runs four simulations per step; it should finish in about two seconds if the simulations are cheap, though the hoop simulation may be heavier. The full attract loop spans ten activities up to fourteen seconds, totaling eight thousand four hundred frames.\n\nThe attract loop records each activity ID in a set. It stops once the set contains ten unique IDs. The attract index wraps to the first activity after the tenth switch.\n\nEnter during attract returns to the title screen. Eight hundred frames of activity last about thirteen seconds, keeping the mode active instead of entering menu idle. Stepping through settings or title attract without input for seven hundred frames also keeps the mode active. A frozen settings test will verify that attract does not start while the pause menu is open.\n\nThe frozen flag persists when opening settings from pause, so attract stays blocked. The dev slider will loop through TUNABLE_TABS and nudge the focused row; the \"more\" row is skipped. Nudge rounds values to three decimals.\n\nThe balance tab holds eight keys across two pages. The first page has five keys and the second has three, so the test must choose \"more\" to reach the last three. KeyE switches tabs and resets focus without clearing the dirty economy value. After nudging all balance keys, reset-tab restores their defaults while leaving the economy value intact. The simulation object must remain alive after nudge, and its tunables must match the new values.\n\nThe hideHud tool toggles the HUD off and back on. The god tool enables god mode if a simulation exists. The overlay tool toggles overlays. The day tool increments the guest day from one to two. The drips tool adds fifty and updates items. The unlock tool unlocks all content. The auto tool changes the driver. The camera tool sets freeCam to true even if setCamera throws.\n\nLine tool dialogue changes are verified by asserting the spoken array is non-empty and has changed. Sound tool assertions check that songPlaying or a chime is active, and audio counts if present. Anim tool checks that viewerClip appears in the clip list. Export tool parses the JSON output into an object. Wipe tool asserts the guest is null and the save is empty. Time tool cycles timeScale through 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, and 3. Jump tool advances to heron activity and asserts mode is activity. The remaining tests will pause, open dev, and finish.\n\nThe finish screen is on tools page zero, so five KeyE presses from balance reach it. A `tabs` helper will tap KeyE n times to navigate between tabs. The dev tab resets to zero when entering the dev menu, and KeyE still works while timeScale is zero because the update loop continues.\n\nactivateDev returns early for tunable IDs because nudging is handled separately; only tools IDs invoke runTool. The \"more\" option on the tools page increments toolPage. In Node, medley mode has empty rows from refresh, so ArrowRight input is not captured during that activity.\n\nThe device stays keyboard during medley because the stub pads are not seen by engine input. Key holds do not change menus. Pad lanes read directly from PadDriver. Axes must be set before stepping medley. Lane positions diverge after 120 frames.\n\nArrowRight held during medley moves the key lane. Pad axes are set to 0.9 and -0.9. The first pad claims index 0 and the second claims index 1. After three pad claims, focus remains on fill so choose('start') moves it to start.\n\nThe exact pad-claim sequence is now defined: pad 0 claims on the first step, pad 1 on the next, then Enter claims the key. After choosing start, the mode becomes medley with three lanes and the held ArrowRight should move them.\n\nThe first app step initializes prevPad to false. Gamepad objects must include buttons and axes arrays so pad input reads correctly. applyDeadzone preserves a 0.9 axis value. Medley pose events read dot.x for lane position, so the three lanes will diverge after 120 frames.\n\nThe intro is skipped. A separate full bot medley run will not share state with the family test. The hub pulse can be tested without an engine because it does not require fixed steps. A mock hub will record calls and handlers.\n\nThe mock hub records send, progress, and result calls. The start handler triggers openMedley and hubPulse. A 100 ms interval is created for progress updates. Emitting end clears that interval so the test does not hang.\n\nThe test will emit `end` at the end to clear the hub interval and prevent hanging. A `time-up` event is emitted twice; the first call should produce a result, while the second must not, confirming deduplication. The mock hub interval is created after `hubPulse`, so emitting `end` immediately afterward clears it correctly.\n\nA negative hub time during countdown causes tickMedley to return early, so medleyT stays at zero and the ghost does not update. When time is positive, medleyT advances normally and lanes step. Other materials also interpolate shown.x toward their target when the ghost is present.\n\nThe hub test will run on the engine so step works correctly. A try/finally block will ensure end is emitted to clear the progress interval and prevent hanging. The first hubPulse should emit a progress event with score zero. The app source will be checked for Math.random calls outside drivers.js, since BotDriver already has its own fallback.\n\nvoice.js uses a custom hash, noiseBurst uses an LCG, and main.js is also being checked for Math.random. The scan will exclude the fallback line in drivers.js. A `check` helper will collect test names and run assertions. Ready to write test.mjs and tools/shot.mjs.\n\nThe test runner will use an async `test` helper instead of the earlier synchronous check. Each test is awaited and failures are caught and pushed into a list. Section names will not be printed. A `beat` utility using `appendFileSync` and `mkdirSync` is being drafted.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-6441c3f4-f651-4a9c-8895-0325958d82f5-71","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"dispose\\(\\)","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/types/engine/Engine.d.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1955,"output_tokens":4714,"cache_read_input_tokens":139392,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"dcedf547-8ee5-4e3d-bc36-beb1d7d8fcf4"}
