{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-571f039d-17f4-460f-8fc6-fc75b27e9dfb-85","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/** Step, capture the WebGPU frame, composite canvases, return a PNG data URL. */\\nimport { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\n\\nexport function writePng(file, dataUrl) {\\n  writeFileSync(file, Buffer.from(String(dataUrl).split(',')[1], 'base64'));\\n}\\n\\nexport async function grab(page) {\\n  return page.evaluate(async () => {\\n10→    const engine = window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__;\\n    const app = window.__GAME__;\\n    engine.step(20);\\n    let frame;\\n    try {\\n      frame = await engine.renderer.capture();\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      return { error: String(err && err.stack || err) };\\n    }\\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\\n20→    canvas.width = frame.width;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/** Step, capture the WebGPU frame, composite canvases, return a PNG data URL. */\\nimport { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\n\\nexport function writePng(file, dataUrl) {\\n  writeFileSync(file, Buffer.from(String(dataUrl).split(',')[1], 'base64'));\\n}\\n\\nexport async function grab(page) {\\n  return page.evaluate(async () => {\\n10→    const engine = window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__;\\n    const app = window.__GAME__;\\n    engine.step(20);\\n    let frame;\\n    try {\\n      frame = await engine.renderer.capture();\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      return { error: String(err && err.stack || err) };\\n    }\\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\\n20→    canvas.width = frame.width;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/tools/shot.mjs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":20,\"raw_output\":\"/** Step, capture the WebGPU frame, composite canvases, return a PNG data URL. */\\nimport { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\n\\nexport function writePng(file, dataUrl) {\\n  writeFileSync(file, Buffer.from(String(dataUrl).split(',')[1], 'base64'));\\n}\\n\\nexport async function grab(page) {\\n  return page.evaluate(async () => {\\n    const engine = window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__;\\n    const app = window.__GAME__;\\n    engine.step(20);\\n    let frame;\\n    try {\\n      frame = await engine.renderer.capture();\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      return { error: String(err && err.stack || err) };\\n    }\\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\\n    canvas.width = frame.width;\\n\",\"total_lines\":51}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"3e782c54-00ca-4e18-906c-1f6858412dd0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_207","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now examining browser.mjs. A potential issue with grab() calling engine was noted.\n\nConfirmed that stepping 20 frames inside grab() is safe. Title, music, and row signatures remain stable. Both boots should return matching grab values. Page errors during the step will be collected separately.\n\nDrafting browser.mjs. The family hour logic will be duplicated via page.evaluate using engine.step and setKey. A getGamepads stub must be installed before any steps.\n\nA fresh page will be used for family hour to ensure the title screen. The fit() helper checks widget bounds against canvas dimensions and detects horizontal overflow.\n\nThe fit() helper now returns widget bounds, overflow status, and total widget count. The audio analyser code resumes the suspended context, creates a 2048-point analyser connected to the master bus, steps the engine for 30 frames, then presses Enter to trigger playback before capturing.\n\nThe analyser code releases Enter, plays a chime, and computes energy from the time-domain buffer. Sparse music may produce low energy at reduced volume, so the threshold should be small but positive.\n\nReal-time audio scheduling in headless mode requires explicit waits. The analyser code will pause 400 ms before reading the buffer, and play the chime for 100 ms before capturing.\n\nAn offline voice test is being drafted using window.__VOICE__ to syllabify and render a phrase.\n\nThe offline voice test now uses an OfflineAudioContext to render the phrase. It computes RMS amplitude from the channel data and begins pitch detection via autocorrelation.\n\nThe offline voice test now compares the 'ounce' and 'coach' speakers. It asserts that RMS amplitude exceeds 0.02 and that 'ounce' pitch is higher than or equal to 'coach' pitch, using both autocorrelation and mean F0.\n\nAutocorrelation may lock onto harmonics from sawtooth waveforms, causing incorrect pitch readings. Comparing mean scheduled frequencies is a safer way to verify the pitch difference.\n\nAutocorrelation may fail due to sawtooth harmonics. A Goertzel filter at 165 Hz and 340 Hz will be used instead. The energy at each frequency will be computed by summing the real and imaginary parts of the sampled data.\n\nThe Goertzel pitch comparison is now the chosen method. The gamepad stub will return a connected pad with all axes at zero and the first button pressed.\n\nThe gamepad stub now releases the button after two steps and returns the prompt. The engine maps button 0 to PadCross, so wasPressed should set the device to pad. After overriding getGamepads, a step will let the engine pick up the new pad state. For the Xbox test, the pad button must be pressed again after navigating to glyphs because the last key step may have switched the device back to keyboard.\n\nThe key test will press Enter and check that the prompt includes Enter. The mouse test will move the pointer to a widget and step; if the prompt is not Click, it will try a larger move or fail. Hovering requires a non-focused widget, so the title screen must be left first. The inverse NDC conversion for the row-about widget is being drafted.\n\nThe device test sequence is now fixed: capture the title screen first, then gamepad, Xbox glyphs, key, mouse move, and finally hover and click. A helper function will open a fresh page, boot the game, grab the initial frame, close the page, and return the signature for comparison across two boots. The Xbox navigation test may switch to settings mode, which is acceptable after the initial capture.\n\nThe two-boot signature test will record the initial grab first, then close the first page and boot a second page for interaction. Viewport tests will resize the page to 1920, 1280, and 640 pixels and step once after each resize to let the engine update its art. The planned shots are title, activity, island, and pause at 1920 resolution. Switching the guest driver to expert is difficult because freshGuest is not easily accessible inside page.evaluate.\n\nThe activity shot will use `app.go('activity','tree')` with no guest, then step 40 frames and grab. The island shot will call `go('island')` directly, since the mode sets without a guest and page 0 stations do not need one. The pause shot will trigger from activity via KeyP and dev, then grab. Webkit and Firefox headless browsers are being prepared for testing.\n\nA headless browser test will open the game page and assert that the body text contains \"Failed to start: WebGPU unavailable\" while no page errors are recorded. The check will also scan the document element for that message, since it may appear in a heading or canvas rather than inside the body. For WebKit, the test will use an iframe parent page to reproduce the session failure.\n\nThe WebKit session-failure test will use an iframe parent page. A script inside the iframe listens for a \"hello\" message from the parent, replies with a \"welcome\" payload, and records any error posted back into `window.__failed`. The test waits up to 20 seconds for that failure flag or for the body text to contain the expected error. The engine starts quickly on WebKit but throws after the welcome handshake completes.\n\nThe WebKit session-failure test will use an iframe parent page served by the hub server. The parent script listens for a \"hello\" message, replies with a \"welcome\" payload, and records any error posted back into `window.__failed`. This test can run on the hub server itself, or as a separate file:// page before the hub starts.\n\nThe test sequence is now fully ordered. Chromium file boots and suite shots come first, followed by a phone viewport check. WebKit and Firefox file message tests follow, then the browser hub server starts. Four Chromium harness URLs will run with four players, but a failure there may OOM so it will be logged rather than repeated. The WebKit iframe session-failure test will run on the hub server itself. The parent page for that iframe will use an absolute HTTP URL via `page.setContent`. A `Content-Type` fetch will run before the hub starts. Playwright device names also need handling.\n\nThe iPhone viewport will fall back to the first available iPhone model if 'iPhone 13' is missing. Logs will be written to browser.log, hub.log, nosession.log, and capture-error.txt. File boots must report zero page errors. The viewport will be set to 1280 by 720 pixels for a 16:9 aspect ratio. Music volume will be checked as exactly 0.1 after waiting for __GAME__.\n\nChromium waits for `window.__GAME__` and `window.__PIXEL_ENGINE__` to appear before proceeding, timing out if WebGPU fails. WebKit skips this wait and instead checks the body text directly. The engine start catch block suppresses rethrowing, so a failed start leaves the page open with the error message in the body. A console listener will record errors to the log without failing the test.\n\nThe browser test script is being written now. Chromium will ignore console.error messages that report a failed start, since those may not trigger pageerror. WebKit will still check the body text directly for that error.\n\nThe name-length test will backspace twice from \"Guest\" and append \"ada\" to produce \"Gueada\". The buffer is not lowercased by freshGuest; typeName handles lowercasing when adding characters. Both typeName and menu must see Backspace on the down frame, and typeName slices the first character.\n\nThe name-input logic is confirmed: Backspace during editing blocks the backspace action, and Enter activates the done button. The week test flow is verified: after a reload, the game returns to the title screen, so choosing start from there correctly proceeds to weigh mode. The expert driver must be set after reload because the new App instance lacks one.\n\nThe first weigh runs as human before the driver is set, then gets discarded and reweighed. The second weigh also starts as human, so the expert driver is applied after that weigh finishes. Island mode follows the second question, with the driver set after the onward transition.\n\nThe activity test reassigns `app` to the reloaded instance after `loadGame`, so `play()` correctly uses that fresh app. The first activity test calls `loadGame`, which replaces the initial world, while the title boot test leaves it untouched.\n\nThe dev test boots the game again. The attract test passes a session into boot, so loadGame sees it and skips island mode; the game stays on the card for 700 steps. The touch boot test sets touchOnly to true, keeping the title screen until Enter clears it. The family test boots the title and menus with a navigator stub, though a Node.js navigator getter might throw on assignment. The hub test boots the game and then binds the hub.\n\nThe hub test needs a try/finally block to clear the interval. If an assertion throws before emit end, the interval leaks and Node hangs. The test wrapper does not provide a finally clause.\n\nThe hub test will be patched with try/finally to clear the interval and prevent the leaked pulse from interfering with later tests. The medley test is long but safely nested inside the host try block. Family and medley can run on the same app because `go` replaces lanes, and bot medley does not read pads so stubbing them remains safe.\n\nThe hub test will be patched with a finally block to clear the interval. The browser script is next. Dev-tools choose must handle rows that are already focused. The reset-tab sequence moves from Economy to Balance by pressing KeyQ four times. It then presses Enter on the reset-tab button. That button may not be focused so arrow keys will move there first. Nudging every key on the Economy tab dirties dripRate. Resetting the balance tab does not clear economy values. The test asserts that dripRate matches the dirty value.\n\nAfter reset-all the tab remains on Balance, so the next step is to press KeyE until Tools is reached. The day tool changes the existing guest created by freshGuest Dev. After a jump leaves to the activity heron, resetting the tab to Balance and pressing KeyE to open Tools lets finish be chosen; it exists, sets finishPick to 1, and awards one star. The first jump press sets jumpPick to 1 and selects ACTIVITIES[1], which is asserted.\n\nA potential bug was found in the dev tools: pressing KeyE to switch tabs resets `devTab` but leaves `toolPage` unchanged. After navigating through the time tool on page 1, the next KeyE press keeps the cursor on the same tool page rather than resetting it to 0. The Tools menu layout is being traced to confirm the exact page positions.\n\nThe Tools menu layout is being traced to confirm the exact page positions. The test order navigates through pages 1 and 2, but export is not on page 2; that page contains camera, line, sound, anim, wipe, and more. This means the test must press export from page 3 instead.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-d064a62a-5eef-4018-bff3-ae8e46acee13-86","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"choose\\(host, app, '","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221607__x6981e3/work/Mint Dial-Wii Fit-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-221607/tools/test.mjs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":356,"output_tokens":5110,"cache_read_input_tokens":171776,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa22-c3bd-7423-a5f1-ddadc552f469","uuid":"2c4c2e21-c115-439c-aa06-ce7671de505b"}
